            # Why Should You Learn This?
            
            One of the first confusing things every Kubernetes engineer notices is this:
            
            Pods are created using:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: v1
            kind: Pod
            ```
            
            But Deployments are created using:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: apps/v1
            kind: Deployment
            ```
            
            Services:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: v1
            ```
            
            Ingress:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
            ```
            
            CronJob:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: batch/v1
            ```
            
            Why are they different?
            
            Why isn't everything simply:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: v1
            ```
            
            To answer that, we need to understand API Groups.
            
            ---
            
            # Before API Groups
            
            Imagine Kubernetes version 1.0 only had a few resources.
            
            ```text
            Pods
            
            Services
            
            ReplicationControllers
            
            Namespaces
            
            Nodes
            ```
            
            Everything lived under:
            
            ```text
            /api/v1
            ```
            
            Simple.
            
            But Kubernetes didn't stay small.
            
            Every release added new resources.
            
            Soon there were:
            
            ```text
            Deployments
            
            ReplicaSets
            
            DaemonSets
            
            StatefulSets
            
            Jobs
            
            CronJobs
            
            Ingress
            
            NetworkPolicies
            
            CSI
            
            RuntimeClasses
            
            Leases
            
            PodDisruptionBudgets
            
            PriorityClasses
            ```
            
            Imagine hundreds of resources all inside:
            
            ```text
            /api/v1
            ```
            
            Problems started appearing.
            
            ---
            
            # The Problems
            
            Imagine every resource existed inside one API.
            
            ```text
            /api/v1
            
            Pods
            
            Deployments
            
            Jobs
            
            Ingress
            
            NetworkPolicy
            
            StorageClass
            
            Lease
            
            RuntimeClass
            
            VolumeSnapshot
            
            Gateway
            ```
            
            Questions become difficult:
            
            - Which team owns which API?
            - How do we version resources independently?
            - What happens if two resources have the same name?
            - How do we deprecate one API without affecting others?
            - How do external projects add APIs?
            
            One giant API becomes impossible to maintain.
            
            ---
            
            # Kubernetes Solution
            
            Kubernetes introduced **API Groups**.
            
            Think of API Groups like folders on your computer.
            
            Instead of putting every file in one folder:
            
            ```text
            Desktop
            
            document.docx
            
            movie.mp4
            
            photo.jpg
            
            music.mp3
            
            notes.txt
            ```
            
            You organize them.
            
            ```text
            Documents/
            
            Photos/
            
            Videos/
            
            Music/
            ```
            
            Kubernetes does exactly the same.
            
            ---
            
            # What is an API Group?
            
            An API Group is a logical collection of related Kubernetes resources.
            
            Instead of one huge API,
            
            Kubernetes separates APIs by functionality.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Core Resources
            
            ↓
            
            Core API Group
            ```
            
            Applications
            
            ↓
            
            ```text
            apps
            ```
            
            Networking
            
            ↓
            
            ```text
            networking.k8s.io
            ```
            
            Batch Processing
            
            ↓
            
            ```text
            batch
            ```
            
            Storage
            
            ↓
            
            ```text
            storage.k8s.io
            ```
            
            Scheduling
            
            ↓
            
            ```text
            scheduling.k8s.io
            ```
            
            Each group owns its own resources.
            
            ---
            
            # Kubernetes API Structure
            
            At a high level:
            
            ```text
            Kubernetes API
            
            ├── Core API Group
            │
            ├── apps
            │
            ├── batch
            │
            ├── networking.k8s.io
            │
            ├── storage.k8s.io
            │
            ├── autoscaling
            │
            ├── policy
            │
            ├── coordination.k8s.io
            │
            └── many more...
            ```
            
            Every API belongs to one group.
            
            ---
            
            # Core API Group
            
            The **Core API Group** is special.
            
            It is the original Kubernetes API.
            
            Unlike other groups,
            
            it has **no group name**.
            
            That's why Pods use:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: v1
            ```
            
            instead of:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: core/v1
            ```
            
            Internally,
            
            its group name is an empty string:
            
            ```text
            ""
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ## Core API URL
            
            Core resources are accessed using:
            
            ```text
            /api/v1
            ```
            
            Notice:
            
            Not:
            
            ```text
            /apis
            ```
            
            Examples:
            
            Pods
            
            ```text
            /api/v1/pods
            ```
            
            Services
            
            ```text
            /api/v1/services
            ```
            
            Namespaces
            
            ```text
            /api/v1/namespaces
            ```
            
            Secrets
            
            ```text
            /api/v1/secrets
            ```
            
            ConfigMaps
            
            ```text
            /api/v1/configmaps
            ```
            
            Nodes
            
            ```text
            /api/v1/nodes
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ## Common Core Resources
            
            | Resource | API Version |
            |-----------|-------------|
            | Pod | v1 |
            | Service | v1 |
            | Namespace | v1 |
            | Secret | v1 |
            | ConfigMap | v1 |
            | Node | v1 |
            | PersistentVolume | v1 |
            | PersistentVolumeClaim | v1 |
            | Event | v1 |
            
            These are fundamental resources that existed early in Kubernetes.
            
            ---
            
            # Named API Groups
            
            As Kubernetes evolved,
            
            new functionality was placed into **Named API Groups**.
            
            Unlike the Core API Group,
            
            they have explicit names.
            
            Example:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: apps/v1
            ```
            
            Group:
            
            ```text
            apps
            ```
            
            Version:
            
            ```text
            v1
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ## URL Structure
            
            Named API Groups always use:
            
            ```text
            /apis
            ```
            
            Example:
            
            Deployments
            
            ```text
            /apis/apps/v1/deployments
            ```
            
            Jobs
            
            ```text
            /apis/batch/v1/jobs
            ```
            
            Ingress
            
            ```text
            /apis/networking.k8s.io/v1/ingresses
            ```
            
            StorageClass
            
            ```text
            /apis/storage.k8s.io/v1/storageclasses
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Common Named API Groups
            
            ### apps
            
            Resources:
            
            ```text
            Deployment
            
            ReplicaSet
            
            StatefulSet
            
            DaemonSet
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ### batch
            
            Resources:
            
            ```text
            Job
            
            CronJob
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ### networking.k8s.io
            
            Resources:
            
            ```text
            Ingress
            
            NetworkPolicy
            
            IngressClass
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ### autoscaling
            
            Resources:
            
            ```text
            HorizontalPodAutoscaler
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ### storage.k8s.io
            
            Resources:
            
            ```text
            StorageClass
            
            CSIDriver
            
            CSIStorageCapacity
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ### coordination.k8s.io
            
            Resources:
            
            ```text
            Lease
            ```
            
            Used for:
            
            - Leader Election
            - Node Heartbeats
            
            ---
            
            ### policy
            
            Resources:
            
            ```text
            PodDisruptionBudget
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ### scheduling.k8s.io
            
            Resources:
            
            ```text
            PriorityClass
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why API Groups Exist
            
            API Groups solve several important problems.
            
            ---
            
            ## 1. Logical Organization
            
            Instead of:
            
            ```text
            One Huge API
            ```
            
            we get:
            
            ```text
            apps
            
            batch
            
            networking
            
            storage
            
            autoscaling
            ```
            
            Much easier to understand.
            
            ---
            
            ## 2. Independent Versioning
            
            Suppose Deployments need a new API version.
            
            Without API Groups:
            
            Everything would require upgrading.
            
            With API Groups:
            
            Only:
            
            ```text
            apps
            ```
            
            changes.
            
            Other groups remain untouched.
            
            ---
            
            ## Example
            
            Current:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1
            ```
            
            Future:
            
            ```text
            apps/v2
            ```
            
            Storage APIs remain:
            
            ```text
            storage.k8s.io/v1
            ```
            
            Nothing breaks.
            
            ---
            
            ## 3. Independent Development
            
            Different SIGs (Special Interest Groups) own different APIs.
            
            Examples:
            
            SIG Apps
            
            ↓
            
            ```text
            apps
            ```
            
            SIG Network
            
            ↓
            
            ```text
            networking.k8s.io
            ```
            
            SIG Storage
            
            ↓
            
            ```text
            storage.k8s.io
            ```
            
            Each team evolves its API independently.
            
            ---
            
            ## 4. Avoid Naming Conflicts
            
            Imagine two different teams both creating:
            
            ```text
            Policy
            ```
            
            Without groups:
            
            Conflict.
            
            With groups:
            
            ```text
            policy/v1
            
            security.example.com/v1
            ```
            
            No conflict.
            
            ---
            
            ## 5. Extensibility
            
            API Groups allow:
            
            - CRDs
            - Operators
            - Aggregated APIs
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            database.example.com
            ```
            
            or
            
            ```text
            monitoring.coreos.com
            ```
            
            These custom APIs integrate seamlessly into Kubernetes.
            
            ---
            
            # API Registration
            
            How does Kubernetes know which API Groups exist?
            
            The API Server registers them during startup.
            
            Think of registration as creating an index.
            
            ```text
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            Register Groups
            
            ↓
            
            Register Resources
            
            ↓
            
            Expose Endpoints
            ```
            
            Once registered,
            
            clients can discover them.
            
            ---
            
            # API Registration Flow
            
            During startup:
            
            ```text
            kube-apiserver
            
            ↓
            
            Load Built-in APIs
            
            ↓
            
            Register API Groups
            
            ↓
            
            Register Versions
            
            ↓
            
            Register Resources
            
            ↓
            
            Serve HTTP Endpoints
            ```
            
            Now the API is ready.
            
            ---
            
            # API Discovery
            
            Suppose you run:
            
            ```bash
            kubectl api-versions
            ```
            
            Output:
            
            ```text
            v1
            
            apps/v1
            
            batch/v1
            
            networking.k8s.io/v1
            
            autoscaling/v2
            
            storage.k8s.io/v1
            ```
            
            This information comes directly from the API Server's registration table.
            
            ---
            
            Suppose you run:
            
            ```bash
            kubectl api-resources
            ```
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            NAME          SHORTNAMES   APIVERSION
            pods          po           v1
            services      svc          v1
            deployments   deploy       apps/v1
            jobs                       batch/v1
            ingresses     ing          networking.k8s.io/v1
            ```
            
            Again,
            
            this is generated from registered APIs.
            
            ---
            
            # Built-in vs Registered APIs
            
            At runtime,
            
            the API Server maintains something like:
            
            ```text
            Core API Group
            
            ↓
            
            Pods
            
            Services
            
            Secrets
            
            ConfigMaps
            ```
            
            ```text
            apps
            
            ↓
            
            Deployments
            
            ReplicaSets
            
            StatefulSets
            ```
            
            ```text
            batch
            
            ↓
            
            Jobs
            
            CronJobs
            ```
            
            Each group is registered independently.
            
            ---
            
            # How CRDs Fit In
            
            Suppose you create a CRD:
            
            ```text
            RedisCluster
            ```
            
            Group:
            
            ```text
            database.example.com
            ```
            
            Version:
            
            ```text
            v1
            ```
            
            Kubernetes automatically registers:
            
            ```text
            database.example.com/v1
            ```
            
            Now users can execute:
            
            ```bash
            kubectl get redisclusters
            ```
            
            without changing kube-apiserver source code.
            
            ---
            
            # How Aggregated APIs Fit In
            
            Metrics Server registers:
            
            ```text
            metrics.k8s.io
            ```
            
            Prometheus Adapter registers:
            
            ```text
            custom.metrics.k8s.io
            ```
            
            KEDA may expose:
            
            ```text
            external.metrics.k8s.io
            ```
            
            These are also API Groups.
            
            To clients,
            
            they look exactly like built-in groups.
            
            ---
            
            # Complete API Group Hierarchy
            
            ```text
            Kubernetes API
            
            ├── Core API Group
            │     ├── Pods
            │     ├── Services
            │     ├── ConfigMaps
            │     └── Secrets
            │
            ├── apps
            │     ├── Deployment
            │     ├── ReplicaSet
            │     └── StatefulSet
            │
            ├── batch
            │     ├── Job
            │     └── CronJob
            │
            ├── networking.k8s.io
            │     ├── Ingress
            │     └── NetworkPolicy
            │
            ├── storage.k8s.io
            │
            ├── autoscaling
            │
            ├── policy
            │
            ├── coordination.k8s.io
            │
            ├── metrics.k8s.io
            │
            ├── custom.metrics.k8s.io
            │
            └── database.example.com (CRD)
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Real-World Example
            
            Suppose an e-commerce company creates a custom resource:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: ecommerce.company.com/v1
            kind: Order
            ```
            
            The CRD registers:
            
            ```text
            ecommerce.company.com
            ```
            
            Now developers can run:
            
            ```bash
            kubectl get orders
            ```
            
            The experience is identical to working with built-in resources like Pods or Deployments.
            
