            ```markdown
            # Chapter 3 — API Versions
            
            > **Goal:** Understand why Kubernetes versions its APIs, how Alpha, Beta, and Stable APIs evolve, what Internal and Storage Versions are, and how Kubernetes converts objects between versions without breaking compatibility.
            >
            > By the end of this chapter, you'll understand:
            >
            > - Why API versioning exists
            > - Alpha APIs
            > - Beta APIs
            > - Stable APIs
            > - Internal API Version
            > - Storage Version
            > - Version Conversion
            > - Backward Compatibility
            
            ---
            
            # Why Should You Learn This?
            
            One of the most common interview questions is:
            
            > **Why does Kubernetes have `apps/v1`, `autoscaling/v2`, and `batch/v1` instead of just `v1`?**
            
            Or,
            
            > **How can Kubernetes upgrade APIs without breaking existing applications?**
            
            The answer is **API Versioning**.
            
            Without versioning:
            
            - Kubernetes upgrades would constantly break existing YAML files.
            - Older clients couldn't communicate with newer clusters.
            - New features couldn't be introduced safely.
            - Backward compatibility would be impossible.
            
            API Versioning is one of the core reasons Kubernetes has remained stable while evolving rapidly.
            
            ---
            
            # The Problem Before API Versioning
            
            Imagine Kubernetes had only one API.
            
            ```text
            /apps
            
            ↓
            
            Deployment
            ```
            
            Suppose version 1 of Deployment looks like:
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              replicas: 3
            ```
            
            Later, Kubernetes developers decide to redesign Deployments.
            
            The new version becomes:
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              scaling:
                replicas: 3
            ```
            
            Now every existing Deployment YAML breaks.
            
            Thousands of production clusters would fail after upgrading.
            
            This is unacceptable.
            
            ---
            
            # Kubernetes Solution
            
            Instead of replacing APIs,
            
            Kubernetes introduces **new API Versions**.
            
            Old APIs continue working until they are officially deprecated.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1beta1
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1beta2
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1
            ```
            
            Every version can coexist for a period of time.
            
            This allows users to migrate gradually.
            
            ---
            
            # What is an API Version?
            
            An API Version defines the schema and behavior of a Kubernetes resource at a particular point in time.
            
            Example:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: apps/v1
            kind: Deployment
            ```
            
            Here:
            
            Group:
            
            ```text
            apps
            ```
            
            Version:
            
            ```text
            v1
            ```
            
            The API Server knows exactly which schema to use.
            
            ---
            
            # API Version Format
            
            The format is:
            
            ```text
            <Group>/<Version>
            ```
            
            Examples:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1
            
            batch/v1
            
            autoscaling/v2
            
            networking.k8s.io/v1
            ```
            
            The Core API Group is special.
            
            It omits the group name:
            
            ```text
            v1
            ```
            
            instead of:
            
            ```text
            core/v1
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Kubernetes API Lifecycle
            
            Every Kubernetes API generally progresses through three stages.
            
            ```text
            Alpha
            
            ↓
            
            Beta
            
            ↓
            
            Stable (GA)
            ```
            
            Not every API reaches Stable.
            
            Some are removed before becoming stable.
            
            ---
            
            # Alpha APIs
            
            Alpha APIs represent the earliest development stage.
            
            Characteristics:
            
            - Experimental
            - Feature incomplete
            - May change at any time
            - Disabled by default (usually behind Feature Gates)
            - Not recommended for production
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1alpha1
            ```
            
            Developers use Alpha APIs to gather community feedback.
            
            ---
            
            ## Why Alpha Exists
            
            Imagine Kubernetes introduces a new scheduling feature.
            
            Nobody knows:
            
            - Is the design correct?
            - Does it scale?
            - Is the API intuitive?
            - Are there security concerns?
            
            Instead of making it permanent,
            
            developers release an Alpha version first.
            
            This allows experimentation without committing to long-term support.
            
            ---
            
            # Beta APIs
            
            After Alpha matures,
            
            the API moves to Beta.
            
            Characteristics:
            
            - Mostly complete
            - More stable
            - Enabled by default in many cases
            - Backward compatibility is expected
            - Suitable for testing and some production workloads
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            autoscaling/v2beta2
            ```
            
            Beta APIs receive extensive testing before becoming stable.
            
            ---
            
            # Stable APIs (GA)
            
            GA stands for:
            
            ```text
            General Availability
            ```
            
            Stable APIs are production-ready.
            
            Characteristics:
            
            - Fully supported
            - Backward compatible
            - Long-term maintenance
            - Recommended for production
            - Breaking changes are avoided
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1
            ```
            
            Most production clusters use Stable APIs.
            
            ---
            
            # API Lifecycle Example
            
            Let's imagine a fictional API.
            
            ```text
            v1alpha1
            
            ↓
            
            v1alpha2
            
            ↓
            
            v1beta1
            
            ↓
            
            v1beta2
            
            ↓
            
            v1
            ```
            
            Each stage introduces improvements while maintaining a migration path.
            
            ---
            
            # Real Kubernetes Examples
            
            Deployment:
            
            ```text
            extensions/v1beta1
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1beta1
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1beta2
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1
            ```
            
            Ingress:
            
            ```text
            extensions/v1beta1
            
            ↓
            
            networking.k8s.io/v1beta1
            
            ↓
            
            networking.k8s.io/v1
            ```
            
            CronJob:
            
            ```text
            batch/v2alpha1
            
            ↓
            
            batch/v1beta1
            
            ↓
            
            batch/v1
            ```
            
            Notice that APIs may even move between API Groups as Kubernetes evolves.
            
            ---
            
            # Internal API Version
            
            Everything we've discussed so far concerns **external versions**.
            
            But inside the API Server,
            
            Kubernetes uses a different representation.
            
            This is called the **Internal Version**.
            
            Think of it as Kubernetes' native language.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Does Kubernetes Need an Internal Version?
            
            Suppose the API Server receives:
            
            ```yaml
            apps/v1
            ```
            
            Later, another client sends:
            
            ```yaml
            apps/v1beta2
            ```
            
            Internally,
            
            the API Server does **not** maintain separate code paths for every version.
            
            Instead,
            
            everything is converted into a single internal object.
            
            ```text
            apps/v1
            
            ↓
            
            Internal Deployment Object
            
            ↓
            
            Business Logic
            
            ↓
            
            Storage
            ```
            
            Likewise,
            
            ```text
            apps/v1beta2
            
            ↓
            
            Internal Deployment Object
            
            ↓
            
            Business Logic
            
            ↓
            
            Storage
            ```
            
            The API Server only needs to understand one internal representation.
            
            ---
            
            # Internal Conversion Flow
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1
            
            ↓
            
            Conversion
            
            ↓
            
            Internal Version
            
            ↓
            
            Validation
            
            ↓
            
            Admission
            
            ↓
            
            Storage
            ```
            
            Every request eventually becomes the internal object.
            
            ---
            
            # Storage Version
            
            The API Version used to store objects in etcd is called the **Storage Version**.
            
            This is important because:
            
            Users may submit different API versions,
            
            but Kubernetes stores objects in one consistent format.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Suppose a client sends:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: apps/v1beta2
            ```
            
            The API Server converts it:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1beta2
            
            ↓
            
            Internal Version
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1 (Storage Version)
            
            ↓
            
            etcd
            ```
            
            Another client later requests:
            
            ```yaml
            apps/v1beta2
            ```
            
            The API Server performs the reverse conversion:
            
            ```text
            Storage Version
            
            ↓
            
            Internal Version
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1beta2
            
            ↓
            
            Response
            ```
            
            The client never knows the object is stored differently.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Storage Version Matters
            
            Imagine a cluster with:
            
            - 50 different clients
            - Multiple Kubernetes versions
            - Thousands of objects
            
            If every object were stored in different formats,
            
            etcd would become extremely complex.
            
            Instead,
            
            everything is stored using one Storage Version.
            
            Benefits:
            
            - Simpler storage
            - Faster upgrades
            - Easier migration
            - Consistent persistence
            
            ---
            
            # Version Conversion
            
            Version Conversion is one of Kubernetes' most powerful features.
            
            It allows:
            
            ```text
            Old Client
            
            ↓
            
            New Cluster
            
            ↓
            
            Works
            ```
            
            and
            
            ```text
            New Client
            
            ↓
            
            Old Stored Objects
            
            ↓
            
            Works
            ```
            
            without breaking compatibility.
            
            ---
            
            # Conversion Workflow
            
            Suppose:
            
            Stored object:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1
            ```
            
            Client requests:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1beta2
            ```
            
            Flow:
            
            ```text
            etcd
            
            ↓
            
            Storage Version
            
            ↓
            
            Internal Version
            
            ↓
            
            Version Converter
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1beta2
            
            ↓
            
            Response
            ```
            
            The API Server performs this automatically.
            
            ---
            
            # Conversion in the Opposite Direction
            
            Suppose:
            
            Client submits:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1beta2
            ```
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            apps/v1beta2
            
            ↓
            
            Internal Version
            
            ↓
            
            Storage Version
            
            ↓
            
            etcd
            ```
            
            Again,
            
            the API Server handles the conversion transparently.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Conversion is Important
            
            Imagine upgrading Kubernetes.
            
            Before:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1beta1
            ```
            
            After:
            
            ```text
            apps/v1
            ```
            
            Without conversion:
            
            Every YAML file must be rewritten immediately.
            
            With conversion:
            
            Older clients continue working during the migration period.
            
            This minimizes downtime and simplifies upgrades.
            
            ---
            
            # API Deprecation
            
            Eventually,
            
            older API Versions are removed.
            
            Typical lifecycle:
            
            ```text
            v1alpha1
            
            ↓
            
            v1beta1
            
            ↓
            
            v1
            
            ↓
            
            Deprecated
            
            ↓
            
            Removed
            ```
            
            Before removal,
            
            Kubernetes provides deprecation warnings so users have time to migrate.
            
            ---
            
            # Complete Version Flow
            
            Suppose a client sends:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: apps/v1
            kind: Deployment
            ```
            
            Complete journey:
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            HTTPS Request
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            Version Detection
            
            ↓
            
            Convert to Internal Version
            
            ↓
            
            Authentication
            
            ↓
            
            Authorization
            
            ↓
            
            Admission
            
            ↓
            
            Validation
            
            ↓
            
            Convert to Storage Version
            
            ↓
            
            etcd
            
            ↓
            
            Stored
            ```
            
            When retrieving:
            
            ```text
            etcd
            
            ↓
            
            Storage Version
            
            ↓
            
            Internal Version
            
            ↓
            
            Requested API Version
            
            ↓
            
            Client
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Real-World Example
            
            Suppose your company upgrades Kubernetes.
            
            Before upgrade:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: batch/v1beta1
            kind: CronJob
            ```
            
            After upgrading,
            
            the Storage Version becomes:
            
            ```text
            batch/v1
            ```
            
            Existing objects are still readable because the API Server converts between versions automatically.
            
            Eventually,
            
            you update your manifests to:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: batch/v1
            ```
            
            before the Beta API is removed.
            
            ---
            
            # Common Interview Questions
            
            ### Q1. Why does Kubernetes version its APIs?
            
            To allow APIs to evolve without breaking existing clients or workloads.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q2. What is an Alpha API?
            
            An experimental API intended for testing and feedback. It may change or be removed at any time.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q3. What is a Beta API?
            
            A mostly stable API with broader testing. It is closer to production readiness but may still change before GA.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q4. What is a Stable (GA) API?
            
            A production-ready API with strong backward compatibility guarantees and long-term support.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q5. What is the Internal Version?
            
            The Internal Version is Kubernetes' in-memory representation of an object used by the API Server for processing.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q6. What is the Storage Version?
            
            The Storage Version is the API version used to persist objects in etcd.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q7. Why doesn't Kubernetes store every API version in etcd?
            
            Maintaining multiple storage formats would complicate persistence and upgrades. A single Storage Version keeps storage consistent.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q8. What is Version Conversion?
            
            Version Conversion is the process of translating objects between external API versions, the Internal Version, and the Storage Version.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q9. Can two clients use different API versions simultaneously?
            
            Yes.
            
            The API Server converts objects as needed so different supported API versions can coexist.
            
            ---
            
            ### Q10. What happens when an API Version is removed?
            
            Clients using the removed version receive errors and must migrate to a supported version.
            
            ---
            
            # Troubleshooting Guide
            
            | Problem | Likely Cause |
            |----------|--------------|
            | `no matches for kind` | API Version removed or unsupported |
            | Deprecation warning | API Version is scheduled for removal |
            | Manifest rejected after upgrade | Deprecated API Version no longer available |
            | Conversion webhook failure (CRDs) | Custom conversion logic misconfigured |
            | Incompatible client | Client requests an unsupported API Version |
            
