            # Backend Quota
            
            The backend database has a configured size limit.
            
            This limit is called the:
            
            ```text
            Backend Quota
            ```
            
            Purpose:
            
            Prevent uncontrolled database growth.
            
            ---
            
            # Why a Backend Quota Exists
            
            Without limits:
            
            ```text
            Continuous Writes
            
            ↓
            
            Database Grows
            
            ↓
            
            Disk Full
            
            ↓
            
            Cluster Failure
            ```
            
            Quota protects the cluster before storage is exhausted.
            
            ---
            
            # Backend Quota Workflow
            
            ```text
            Writes
            
            ↓
            
            Database Grows
            
            ↓
            
            Quota Approached
            
            ↓
            
            Warning
            
            ↓
            
            Writes Eventually Rejected
            ```
            
            Operators must respond before the limit is reached.
            
            ---
            
            # Large Database Effects
            
            Very large databases increase:
            
            - Startup time
            - Backup duration
            - Defragmentation time
            - Restore time
            - Memory requirements
            
            Keeping the backend reasonably sized improves operational efficiency.
            
            ---
            
            # WAL (Write-Ahead Log)
            
            Before modifying BoltDB,
            
            every write is first recorded in the:
            
            ```text
            WAL
            
            Write-Ahead Log
            ```
            
            Remember:
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Leader
            
            ↓
            
            WAL
            
            ↓
            
            fsync
            
            ↓
            
            Commit
            ```
            
            The WAL protects durability.
            
            ---
            
            # Why WAL Performance Matters
            
            The WAL performs:
            
            ```text
            Synchronous
            
            Disk Writes
            ```
            
            If storage is slow,
            
            every transaction waits.
            
            Therefore:
            
            ```text
            Fast WAL
            
            ↓
            
            Fast Cluster
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # WAL Storage
            
            Production recommendations:
            
            ```text
            Low Latency SSD
            
            ↓
            
            Fast fsync
            
            ↓
            
            Stable Power
            
            ↓
            
            Reliable Filesystem
            ```
            
            Poor storage causes commit latency to increase dramatically.
            
            ---
            
            # WAL Bottleneck
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Write Request
            
            ↓
            
            WAL fsync
            
            ↓
            
            Wait
            
            ↓
            
            Commit
            ```
            
            Most write latency begins here.
            
            ---
            
            # Snapshot Count
            
            RAFT periodically creates snapshots.
            
            This is different from the disaster recovery snapshots discussed earlier.
            
            RAFT snapshots help reduce:
            
            ```text
            Large WAL
            
            ↓
            
            Replay Time
            
            ↓
            
            Startup Time
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why Snapshot Count Matters
            
            Imagine:
            
            ```text
            10 Million WAL Entries
            ```
            
            Restart.
            
            Without snapshots:
            
            ```text
            Replay
            
            10 Million Entries
            ```
            
            Very slow.
            
            Snapshots reduce replay work.
            
            ---
            
            # Too Small a Snapshot Count
            
            If snapshots occur too frequently:
            
            ```text
            Extra Snapshot Work
            
            ↓
            
            Higher CPU
            
            ↓
            
            Higher Disk Activity
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Too Large a Snapshot Count
            
            If snapshots are too infrequent:
            
            ```text
            Huge WAL
            
            ↓
            
            Long Recovery
            
            ↓
            
            Long Restart
            ```
            
            Balance is important.
            
            ---
            
            # Snapshot Lifecycle
            
            ```text
            Writes
            
            ↓
            
            WAL Grows
            
            ↓
            
            Snapshot Created
            
            ↓
            
            Older WAL Can Be Truncated
            
            ↓
            
            Continue Writing
