            # Before Kubernetes v1.19
            
            Originally,
            
            the Kubernetes Scheduler was largely implemented as one large scheduling algorithm.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler
            
            ↓
            
            Huge Scheduling Logic
            
            ↓
            
            Node
            ```
            
            Everything happened inside one tightly coupled implementation.
            
            ---
            
            # Problems with the Old Design
            
            Imagine adding support for:
            
            - GPUs
            - AI workloads
            - NUMA awareness
            - Custom hardware
            - Internal company policies
            
            Developers had to modify:
            
            ```text
            Core Scheduler Code
            ```
            
            Problems:
            
            - Difficult maintenance
            - Hard to test
            - Hard to extend
            - Vendor patches
            - Merge conflicts
            - Upgrade complexity
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Suppose NVIDIA wants:
            
            ```text
            GPU Scheduling
            ```
            
            Google wants:
            
            ```text
            TPU Scheduling
            ```
            
            A bank wants:
            
            ```text
            Compliance Scheduling
            ```
            
            A telecom company wants:
            
            ```text
            Low-Latency Scheduling
            ```
            
            Without plugins,
            
            every feature requires modifying:
            
            ```text
            Scheduler Core
            ```
            
            Not scalable.
            
            ---
            
            # Why the Scheduling Framework Exists
            
            The Scheduling Framework solves one major problem:
            
            > **Allow scheduling behavior to be extended without rewriting the Scheduler itself.**
            
            Instead of changing Kubernetes,
            
            developers add:
            
            ```text
            Plugins
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Modern Scheduler
            
            Today,
            
            the Scheduler is:
            
            ```text
            Scheduler
            
            ↓
            
            Framework
            
            ↓
            
            Plugins
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduling Decision
            ```
            
            The Scheduler coordinates.
            
            Plugins perform the work.
            
            ---
            
            # High-Level Architecture
            
            ```text
                                 Scheduler
            
                                     │
            
                                     ▼
            
                           Scheduling Framework
            
                                     │
            
                  ┌──────────────────┼──────────────────┐
            
                  ▼                  ▼                  ▼
            
              Built-in          Custom Plugin      Third-Party
            
                 Plugin              Plugin            Plugin
            ```
            
            Everything passes through the framework.
            
            ---
            
            # What is the Scheduling Framework?
            
            The Scheduling Framework is:
            
            > **A plugin execution engine that manages every scheduling decision.**
            
            Think of it as:
            
            ```text
            Operating System
            
            ↓
            
            Applications
            ```
            
            Or:
            
            ```text
            Web Server
            
            ↓
            
            Middleware
            ```
            
            The Framework doesn't decide.
            
            Plugins decide.
            
            ---
            
            # Real-Life Analogy
            
            Imagine airport security.
            
            Passenger arrives.
            
            ```text
            Passenger
            
            ↓
            
            Identity Check
            
            ↓
            
            Luggage Scan
            
            ↓
            
            Immigration
            
            ↓
            
            Boarding
            
            ↓
            
            Aircraft
            ```
            
            Each checkpoint performs one job.
            
            Scheduling works similarly.
            
            ---
            
            # Scheduling Pipeline
            
            ```text
            Pod
            
            ↓
            
            PreFilter
            
            ↓
            
            Filter
            
            ↓
            
            PostFilter
            
            ↓
            
            PreScore
            
            ↓
            
            Score
            
            ↓
            
            Reserve
            
            ↓
            
            Permit
            
            ↓
            
            PreBind
            
            ↓
            
            Bind
            
            ↓
            
            PostBind
            ```
            
            Each stage is called an:
            
            ```text
            Extension Point
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What is an Extension Point?
            
            An Extension Point is:
            
            > **A predefined stage in the scheduling pipeline where one or more plugins can execute.**
            
            Instead of modifying Kubernetes,
            
            developers insert plugins into these stages.
            
            ---
            
            # Extension Point Overview
            
            ```text
            Scheduling Framework
            
            ├── QueueSort
            ├── PreFilter
            ├── Filter
            ├── PostFilter
            ├── PreScore
            ├── Score
            ├── Reserve
            ├── Permit
            ├── PreBind
            ├── Bind
            └── PostBind
            ```
            
            Each serves a different purpose.
            
            ---
            
            # Scheduling Plugins
            
            A Scheduling Plugin is:
            
            > **A modular component implementing one or more scheduling interfaces.**
            
            Example plugins:
            
            ```text
            NodeResourcesFit
            
            NodeAffinity
            
            ImageLocality
            
            TaintToleration
            
            VolumeBinding
            
            PodTopologySpread
            ```
            
            Each plugin performs one specialized task.
            
            ---
            
            # Plugin Philosophy
            
            Instead of:
            
            ```text
            One Plugin
            
            Does Everything
            ```
            
            Kubernetes prefers:
            
            ```text
            Many Small Plugins
            
            ↓
            
            Single Responsibility
            ```
            
            Exactly like:
            
            - Unix philosophy
            - Kubernetes Controllers
            - Linux kernel modules
            
            ---
            
            # Plugin Categories
            
            Plugins generally belong to:
            
            ```text
            Queue
            
            Filtering
            
            Scoring
            
            Reservation
            
            Binding
            ```
            
            Some plugins participate in multiple phases.
            
            ---
            
            # Built-in Plugins
            
            Examples include:
            
            | Plugin | Responsibility |
            |----------|----------------|
            | NodeResourcesFit | CPU/Memory resource validation |
            | NodeAffinity | Node affinity rules |
            | TaintToleration | Taints and tolerations |
            | ImageLocality | Prefer nodes with cached images |
            | PodTopologySpread | Even distribution |
            | InterPodAffinity | Pod affinity and anti-affinity |
            | VolumeBinding | Persistent volume constraints |
            
            ---
            
            # Plugin Registration
            
            When the Scheduler starts,
            
            it loads plugins.
            
            ```text
            Scheduler
            
            ↓
            
            Framework
            
            ↓
            
            Register Plugin
            
            ↓
            
            Ready
            ```
            
            Only registered plugins participate.
            
            ---
            
            # Framework Handle
            
            Plugins occasionally need information.
            
            Instead of directly accessing Scheduler internals,
            
            they receive:
            
            ```text
            Framework Handle
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What is Framework Handle?
            
            Framework Handle is an interface exposing Scheduler services.
            
            It allows plugins to access:
            
            - Scheduler Cache
            - Shared Informers
            - ClientSet
            - Event Recorder
            - Shared Listers
            - Waiting Pods
            - Snapshot Data
            
            without tightly coupling to Scheduler implementation.
            
            ---
            
            # Framework Handle Diagram
            
            ```text
            Plugin
            
            ↓
            
            Framework Handle
            
            ├── Cache
            ├── Client
            ├── Informers
            ├── Event Recorder
            ├── Snapshot
            └── Shared Listers
            ```
            
            Think of it as a toolbox.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Not Give Plugins Direct Access?
            
            Direct access would create:
            
            ```text
            Plugin
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler Internals
            ```
            
            Problems:
            
            - Tight coupling
            - Version dependency
            - Hard upgrades
            - Hard testing
            
            Framework Handle provides abstraction.
            
            ---
            
            # Scheduler Snapshot
            
            Before scheduling begins,
            
            the Framework builds a consistent snapshot.
            
            ```text
            Scheduler Cache
            
            ↓
            
            Snapshot
            
            ↓
            
            Plugins
            ```
            
            All plugins work from the same cluster view.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Snapshot?
            
            Imagine:
            
            Plugin A sees:
            
            ```text
            Node
            
            4 CPU Free
            ```
            
            Plugin B sees:
            
            ```text
            Node
            
            2 CPU Free
            ```
            
            Different data.
            
            Scheduling becomes inconsistent.
            
            Snapshots guarantee consistency.
            
            ---
            
            # Framework Pipeline
            
            The Scheduling Framework executes plugins in a fixed pipeline.
            
            ```text
            QueueSort
            
            ↓
            
            PreFilter
            
            ↓
            
            Filter
            
            ↓
            
            PostFilter
            
            ↓
            
            PreScore
            
            ↓
            
            Score
            
            ↓
            
            Reserve
            
            ↓
            
            Permit
            
            ↓
            
            PreBind
            
            ↓
            
            Bind
            
            ↓
            
            PostBind
            ```
            
            Each stage has a specific purpose.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 1 — QueueSort
            
            Purpose:
            
            Determine:
            
            ```text
            Which Pod
            
            Schedules First?
            ```
            
            Example:
            
            High-priority Pod before low-priority Pod.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 2 — PreFilter
            
            Runs before evaluating Nodes.
            
            Typical work:
            
            - Parse Pod
            - Validate requests
            - Precompute expensive calculations
            
            Executed once per Pod.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 3 — Filter
            
            Runs for:
            
            ```text
            Every Candidate Node
            ```
            
            Question:
            
            ```text
            Can
            
            This Pod
            
            Run Here?
            ```
            
            Possible result:
            
            ```text
            Pass
            
            or
            
            Reject
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 4 — PostFilter
            
            Runs only when:
            
            ```text
            No Node
            
            Passed Filter
            ```
            
            Typical responsibility:
            
            ```text
            Preemption
            ```
            
            We'll study this later.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 5 — PreScore
            
            Runs before scoring.
            
            Purpose:
            
            Prepare information required by Score plugins.
            
            Executed once.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 6 — Score
            
            Runs for every remaining Node.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Node A
            
            85
            
            Node B
            
            93
            
            Node C
            
            71
            ```
            
            Highest score wins.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 7 — Reserve
            
            Temporarily reserves resources.
            
            Purpose:
            
            Avoid race conditions while binding.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 8 — Permit
            
            Optional approval phase.
            
            Can:
            
            ```text
            Approve
            
            Reject
            
            Wait
            ```
            
            Useful for:
            
            Gang Scheduling
            
            Batch Scheduling
            
            AI workloads
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 9 — PreBind
            
            Runs immediately before binding.
            
            Final validation.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 10 — Bind
            
            Assigns:
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              nodeName: worker-3
            ```
            
            The Pod is officially scheduled.
            
            ---
            
            # Stage 11 — PostBind
            
            Executed after successful binding.
            
            Typical usage:
            
            - Metrics
            - Logging
            - Notifications
            - Cleanup
            
            ---
            
            # Complete Pipeline
            
            ```text
            New Pod
            
            ↓
            
            QueueSort
            
            ↓
            
            PreFilter
            
            ↓
            
            Filter
            
            ↓
            
            Candidate Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            PreScore
            
            ↓
            
            Score
            
            ↓
            
            Highest Score
            
            ↓
            
            Reserve
            
            ↓
            
            Permit
            
            ↓
            
            PreBind
            
            ↓
            
            Bind
            
            ↓
            
            PostBind
            
            ↓
            
            Done
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Scheduler Execution Flow
            
            Let's schedule a Pod.
            
            ```yaml
            resources:
              requests:
                cpu: 2
                memory: 4Gi
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 1
            
            Pod enters:
            
            ```text
            Scheduling Queue
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 2
            
            QueueSort decides:
            
            ```text
            Next Pod
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 3
            
            PreFilter computes:
            
            ```text
            CPU
            
            Memory
            
            Affinity
            
            Volumes
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 4
            
            Filter evaluates:
            
            ```text
            Node1
            
            Pass
            
            Node2
            
            Fail
            
            Node3
            
            Pass
            
            Node4
            
            Fail
            ```
            
            Remaining:
            
            ```text
            Node1
            
            Node3
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 5
            
            Score plugins run.
            
            ```text
            Node1
            
            88
            
            Node3
            
            96
            ```
            
            Winner:
            
            ```text
            Node3
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 6
            
            Reserve:
            
            ```text
            Reserve Resources
            
            Node3
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 7
            
            Permit:
            
            ```text
            Approved
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 8
            
            Bind:
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              nodeName: node3
            ```
            
            Scheduling complete.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Multiple Plugins?
            
            Imagine a Pod requiring:
            
            - GPU
            - SSD
            - Same Zone
            - Same Team
            - No Taints
            
            Instead of one algorithm,
            
            plugins collaborate.
            
            ```text
            GPU Plugin
            
            ↓
            
            Affinity Plugin
            
            ↓
            
            Storage Plugin
            
            ↓
            
            Taint Plugin
            
            ↓
            
            Topology Plugin
            ```
            
            Much simpler.
            
            ---
            
            # Plugin Ordering
            
            Ordering matters.
            
            Example:
            
            Correct:
            
            ```text
            Filter
            
            ↓
            
            Score
            
            ↓
            
            Bind
            ```
            
            Incorrect:
            
            ```text
            Score
            
            ↓
            
            Filter
            ```
            
            Scoring invalid Nodes wastes computation.
            
            ---
            
            # Another Ordering Example
            
            Wrong:
            
            ```text
            Bind
            
            ↓
            
            Filter
            ```
            
            Impossible.
            
            Binding requires:
            
            ```text
            Valid Node
            ```
            
            Therefore,
            
            execution order is fixed.
            
            ---
            
            # Parallel Execution
            
            Important optimization.
            
            Some stages execute:
            
            ```text
            Across Nodes
            
            In Parallel
            ```
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            100 Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Filter Plugin
            
            ↓
            
            100 Workers
            
            ↓
            
            Results
            ```
            
            This dramatically improves scheduling throughput.
            
