            # Step 1 — PreBind Plugins
            
            Before Kubernetes binds the Pod,
            
            the Scheduler executes:
            
            ```text
            PreBind
            ```
            
            Purpose:
            
            Perform final validation or preparation.
            
            Examples:
            
            - Volume checks
            - Network reservation
            - External integrations
            - Custom validation
            
            Runs:
            
            ```text
            Once
            
            Per Pod
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why PreBind Exists
            
            Imagine a storage plugin.
            
            The Node was selected successfully.
            
            But:
            
            ```text
            Persistent Volume
            
            ↓
            
            Not Ready
            ```
            
            PreBind can stop the bind before cluster state changes.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            ```text
            Pod
            
            ↓
            
            worker-5
            
            ↓
            
            PreBind
            
            ↓
            
            Volume Available?
            
            ↓
            
            Yes
            
            ↓
            
            Continue
            ```
            
            Otherwise:
            
            ```text
            Binding Failed
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Step 2 — Bind Plugins
            
            Now the Scheduler asks:
            
            > **Who will perform the binding?**
            
            Normally:
            
            ```text
            Default Binder
            ```
            
            But custom Bind plugins may override this behavior.
            
            ---
            
            # Default Binder
            
            Most clusters use the built-in binder.
            
            Its responsibility:
            
            ```text
            Selected Node
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            Bind Pod
            ```
            
            Very simple.
            
            ---
            
            # Bind Plugins
            
            A Bind Plugin implements the logic that performs the final Pod-to-Node assignment.
            
            Examples:
            
            ```text
            DefaultBinder
            
            Custom Cloud Binder
            
            Research Scheduler
            
            Experimental Scheduler
            ```
            
            Only one Bind plugin performs the actual binding.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Custom Bind Plugins?
            
            Some platforms require:
            
            - External resource reservation
            - Bare-metal provisioning
            - HPC systems
            - AI clusters
            - Specialized hardware
            
            A custom binder can integrate with these systems before completing the assignment.
            
            ---
            
            # API Server Binding Request
            
            After the Bind plugin succeeds,
            
            the Scheduler sends a request to the API Server.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Scheduler
            
            ↓
            
            Bind Request
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            ```
            
            The Scheduler **does not** communicate directly with etcd.
            
            Exactly like every other Kubernetes control-plane component,
            
            everything goes through:
            
            ```text
            API Server
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Binding Object
            
            Historically,
            
            Kubernetes exposed a Binding object used to represent this operation.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```yaml
            kind: Binding
            
            Target:
            
            worker-node-3
            
            Object:
            
            frontend-pod
            ```
            
            The API Server processes this binding request and records the assignment.
            
            Although the internal implementation has evolved over Kubernetes releases, the concept remains the same:
            
            > **Associate this Pod with this Node.**
            
            ---
            
            # What Actually Changes?
            
            Before binding:
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              nodeName: ""
            ```
            
            After binding:
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              nodeName: worker-node-3
            ```
            
            This single field changes everything.
            
