            Company requirements:
            
            ```text
            Bachelor's Degree
            
            5 Years Experience
            
            Work Visa
            
            English Speaking
            ```
            
            Applicants are checked.
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Qualified
            
            or
            
            Rejected
            ```
            
            Only qualified candidates move forward.
            
            Filtering works exactly the same way.
            
            ---
            
            # Scheduling Philosophy
            
            Scheduling consists of two steps:
            
            ```text
            Filter
            
            ↓
            
            Who Can Run?
            
            ↓
            
            Score
            
            ↓
            
            Who Should Run?
            ```
            
            Filtering removes impossible Nodes.
            
            Scoring ranks the remaining Nodes.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Cluster:
            
            ```text
            Node1
            
            4 CPU
            
            Node2
            
            8 CPU
            
            Node3
            
            16 CPU
            ```
            
            Pod requires:
            
            ```text
            8 CPU
            ```
            
            Filtering produces:
            
            ```text
            Node1
            
            Rejected
            
            Node2
            
            Accepted
            
            Node3
            
            Accepted
            ```
            
            Scoring hasn't started yet.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Filtering Exists
            
            Imagine skipping filtering.
            
            ```text
            100 Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Score Every Node
            
            ↓
            
            Select Winner
            
            ↓
            
            Oops
            
            Node Doesn't Have Memory
            ```
            
            Waste of computation.
            
            Instead:
            
            ```text
            Filter
            
            ↓
            
            Candidate Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Score
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # High-Level Filter Architecture
            
            ```text
                           Candidate Nodes
            
                                  │
            
                                  ▼
            
                          Filter Plugins
            
                    ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
            
                    ▼             ▼             ▼
            
             Resource Fit   Node Affinity   Volume Check
            
                    ▼             ▼             ▼
            
                 Pass?         Pass?         Pass?
            
                    └─────────────┼─────────────┘
            
                                  ▼
            
                         Remaining Candidate Nodes
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Filter Plugin Execution
            
            Every candidate Node is evaluated.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Node1
            
            Node2
            
            Node3
            
            Node4
            ```
            
            Each plugin executes against every Node.
            
            ---
            
            # Filter Result
            
            Each plugin returns one of two outcomes.
            
            ```text
            Pass
            ```
            
            or
            
            ```text
            Unschedulable
            ```
            
            If **any** Filter Plugin rejects the Node,
            
            that Node is immediately removed.
            
            ---
            
            # Logical AND
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Node
            
            ↓
            
            Plugin A
            
            Pass
            
            ↓
            
            Plugin B
            
            Pass
            
            ↓
            
            Plugin C
            
            Reject
            
            ↓
            
            Node Eliminated
            ```
            
            Every Filter Plugin must agree.
            
            ---
            
            # Parallel Filtering
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            1,000 Nodes
            ```
            
            The Scheduler does **not** evaluate them sequentially.
            
            Instead:
            
            ```text
            1,000 Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Parallel Workers
            
            ↓
            
            Filter Plugins
            
            ↓
            
            Results
            ```
            
            This is one of Kubernetes' biggest scheduling optimizations.
            
            ---
            
            # Built-in Filter Plugins
            
            Modern Kubernetes includes many built-in Filter Plugins.
            
            Examples:
            
            ```text
            NodeResourcesFit
            
            NodeAffinity
            
            TaintToleration
            
            NodePorts
            
            NodeName
            
            VolumeBinding
            
            NodeUnschedulable
            
            InterPodAffinity
            
            PodTopologySpread
            ```
            
            We'll study the major ones.
            
            ---
            
            # NodeResourcesFit
            
            This is one of the most frequently executed Filter Plugins.
            
            Purpose:
            
            ```text
            Can this Node satisfy
            the Pod's resource requests?
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What Does It Check?
            
            Examples:
            
            ```text
            CPU
            
            Memory
            
            Ephemeral Storage
            
            Extended Resources
            
            Huge Pages
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            CPU
            
            4
            
            Memory
            
            8 GB
            ```
            
            Pod requests:
            
            ```yaml
            resources:
              requests:
                cpu: 6
                memory: 2Gi
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Rejected
            
            ↓
            
            Not Enough CPU
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Another Example
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            16 CPU
            
            64 GB
            ```
            
            Pod:
            
            ```text
            4 CPU
            
            8 GB
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Pass
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Requested vs Actual Usage
            
            Very important:
            
            NodeResourcesFit evaluates:
            
            ```text
            Requested Resources
            ```
            
            Not:
            
            ```text
            Actual Runtime Usage
            ```
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Pod Requests
            
            4 CPU
            
            Actually Uses
            
            1 CPU
            ```
            
            Scheduler still reserves:
            
            ```text
            4 CPU
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why?
            
            Because Kubernetes schedules based on guarantees,
            
            not current consumption.
            
            Otherwise:
            
            ```text
            All Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Actual Usage
            
            ↓
            
            Sudden CPU Spike
            
            ↓
            
            Cluster Instability
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # NodeAffinity Plugin
            
            Purpose:
            
            ```text
            Does the Node
            match the Pod's affinity rules?
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Pod:
            
            ```yaml
            nodeSelector:
              region: us-east
            ```
            
            Node:
            
            ```yaml
            labels:
              region: us-west
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Rejected
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Another Example
            
            Pod:
            
            ```yaml
            requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            ```
            
            Requires:
            
            ```text
            ssd=true
            ```
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            ssd=true
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Pass
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why Affinity Matters
            
            Production clusters often dedicate Nodes for:
            
            ```text
            GPU
            
            AI
            
            Database
            
            SSD
            
            High Memory
            
            ARM
            
            Compliance
            ```
            
            Affinity ensures workloads land on appropriate hardware.
            
            ---
            
            # TaintToleration Plugin
            
            Purpose:
            
            ```text
            Can this Pod tolerate
            the Node's taints?
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            gpu=true:NoSchedule
            ```
            
            Pod:
            
            No toleration.
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Rejected
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Matching Example
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            gpu=true:NoSchedule
            ```
            
            Pod:
            
            ```yaml
            tolerations:
            - key: gpu
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Pass
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why Taints Exist
            
            Taints protect Nodes.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Production Database Node
            ```
            
            Without taints:
            
            Any Pod
            
            could schedule there.
            
            With taints:
            
            Only authorized Pods.
            
            ---
            
            # NodePorts (HostPorts) Plugin
            
            Purpose:
            
            ```text
            Does another Pod
            already use
            the requested HostPort?
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            Port 8080
            
            Already Used
            ```
            
            New Pod:
            
            ```yaml
            hostPort: 8080
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Rejected
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why?
            
            HostPorts belong to:
            
            ```text
            The Node
            
            Not
            
            The Pod
            ```
            
            Only one Pod can bind the same HostPort on the same Node.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            HostPort
            
            80
            
            Occupied
            ```
            
            New Pod:
            
            ```yaml
            hostPort: 80
            ```
            
            Scheduler removes that Node.
            
            ---
            
            # NodeName Plugin
            
            Sometimes users explicitly specify:
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              nodeName: worker-5
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What Happens?
            
            Scheduler checks:
            
            ```text
            Current Node
            
            ↓
            
            worker-5?
            
            ↓
            
            Yes
            
            ↓
            
            Pass
            
            No
            
            ↓
            
            Reject
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Important Note
            
            Normally, Pods with `spec.nodeName` already set are **not scheduled** by the default Scheduler because they are considered pre-assigned. The `NodeName` filtering logic is primarily relevant in scheduling flows where a specific target node must be validated.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Use nodeName?
            
            Typical scenarios:
            
            - Debugging
            - Static workloads
            - Custom schedulers
            - Special operational tasks
            
            It is generally **not recommended** for normal application deployments because it bypasses the Scheduler's placement logic.
            
            ---
            
            # VolumeBinding Plugin
            
            Storage constraints matter.
            
            Question:
            
            ```text
            Can the required volume
            be attached
            to this Node?
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Persistent Volume:
            
            ```text
            Zone
            
            us-east-1a
            ```
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            Zone
            
            us-west-2b
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Rejected
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Another Example
            
            Volume:
            
            ```text
            Already Attached
            
            To Another Node
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Rejected
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why Storage Filtering Matters
            
            Without storage validation:
            
            ```text
            Pod Scheduled
            
            ↓
            
            Volume Cannot Mount
            
            ↓
            
            Pod Never Starts
            ```
            
            Filtering prevents invalid placements.
            
            ---
            
            # NodeUnschedulable Plugin
            
            Every Node contains:
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              unschedulable: true
            ```
            
            or
            
            ```yaml
            spec:
              unschedulable: false
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Administrator runs:
            
            ```bash
            kubectl cordon worker-7
            ```
            
            Node becomes:
            
            ```text
            Unschedulable
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Result
            
            Scheduler:
            
            ```text
            Reject
            
            worker-7
            ```
            
            Existing Pods continue running.
            
            New Pods are not placed there.
            
            ---
            
            # Cordon vs Drain
            
            ```text
            Cordon
            
            ↓
            
            No New Pods
            
            Existing Pods Continue
            ```
            
            ```text
            Drain
            
            ↓
            
            Evict Existing Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Node Empty
            ```
            
            NodeUnschedulable only considers the cordon state.
            
            ---
            
            # Filter Execution Order
            
            The Scheduler executes multiple plugins.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Candidate Node
            
            ↓
            
            NodeUnschedulable
            
            ↓
            
            NodeName
            
            ↓
            
            NodeAffinity
            
            ↓
            
            NodeResourcesFit
            
            ↓
            
            VolumeBinding
            
            ↓
            
            NodePorts
            
            ↓
            
            TaintToleration
            
            ↓
            
            Pass?
            ```
            
            **Important:** The exact ordering of built-in Filter Plugins is determined by the Scheduler Profile configuration. The sequence above is illustrative, showing how multiple filters cooperate—not a guaranteed execution order in every Kubernetes release.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Ordering Matters
            
            Suppose:
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            Unschedulable
            ```
            
            No reason to evaluate:
            
            - CPU
            - Memory
            - Volumes
            - Ports
            
            Reject immediately.
            
            Early elimination saves computation.
            
            ---
            
            # Short-Circuit Evaluation
            
            Once a plugin rejects a Node:
            
            ```text
            Node
            
            ↓
            
            Rejected
            
            ↓
            
            Stop Further Checks
            ```
            
            Remaining Filter Plugins do not need to evaluate that Node.
            
            This improves scheduling performance.
            
            ---
            
            # Complete Filtering Workflow
            
            ```text
            Scheduling Snapshot
            
            ↓
            
            Candidate Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Parallel Filter Plugins
            
            ↓
            
            Rejected Nodes Removed
            
            ↓
            
            Remaining Candidate Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            PreScore
            
            ↓
            
            Score
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Production Example 1
            
            Cluster:
            
            ```text
            100 Nodes
            ```
            
            Pod:
            
            ```yaml
            cpu: 8
            memory: 32Gi
            ```
            
            Filter results:
            
            ```text
            100 Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            NodeResourcesFit
            
            ↓
            
            24 Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            NodeAffinity
            
            ↓
            
            18 Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            VolumeBinding
            
            ↓
            
            12 Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            TaintToleration
            
            ↓
            
            10 Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Score Phase
            ```
            
            Filtering dramatically reduces the search space.
            
            ---
            
            # Production Example 2
            
            AI Cluster
            
            Nodes:
            
            ```text
            GPU Nodes
            
            CPU Nodes
            ```
            
            Pod:
            
            ```yaml
            nodeSelector:
              accelerator: nvidia
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            CPU Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Rejected
            
            GPU Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Continue
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Production Example 3
            
            Database Cluster
            
            Nodes:
            
            ```text
            SSD
            
            HDD
            ```
            
            Pod:
            
            ```yaml
            nodeAffinity:
              disk=ssd
            ```
            
            Only SSD Nodes survive filtering.
            
