            # Where Does Preemption Fit?
            
            Normally, scheduling follows this path:
            
            ```text
            Scheduling Queue
            
            ↓
            
            PreFilter
            
            ↓
            
            Filter
            
            ↓
            
            Score
            
            ↓
            
            Reserve
            
            ↓
            
            Bind
            ```
            
            But what happens if **every Node fails filtering?**
            
            Instead of immediately giving up,
            
            the Scheduler may attempt:
            
            ```text
            Preemption
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why Does Preemption Exist?
            
            Imagine a cluster with:
            
            ```text
            10 Nodes
            ```
            
            Every Node is fully utilized.
            
            A new Pod arrives:
            
            ```text
            Priority
            
            1000
            ```
            
            The Scheduler performs filtering.
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Node-1
            
            Rejected
            
            Node-2
            
            Rejected
            
            ...
            
            Node-10
            
            Rejected
            ```
            
            Normally,
            
            the Pod would remain:
            
            ```text
            Pending
            ```
            
            But this Pod is extremely important.
            
            Should Kubernetes simply wait?
            
            No.
            
            Instead,
            
            Kubernetes can reclaim resources.
            
            ---
            
            # Real-World Analogy
            
            Imagine a hospital.
            
            Current operating rooms:
            
            ```text
            Routine Surgery
            
            Routine Surgery
            
            Routine Surgery
            ```
            
            Suddenly,
            
            an emergency patient arrives.
            
            The hospital may postpone:
            
            ```text
            Routine Surgery
            ```
            
            to make room for:
            
            ```text
            Emergency Surgery
            ```
            
            That is exactly what Kubernetes preemption does.
            
            ---
            
            # Simple Definition
            
            Preemption is:
            
            > **The process of evicting lower-priority Pods so that a higher-priority Pod can be scheduled.**
            
            It is a last-resort scheduling mechanism.
            
            ---
            
            # High-Level Architecture
            
            ```text
            High Priority Pod
            
                    │
            
                    ▼
            
            Normal Scheduling
            
                    │
            
                    ▼
            
            No Feasible Node
            
                    │
            
                    ▼
            
            Preemption
            
                    │
            
                    ▼
            
            Victim Selection
            
                    │
            
                    ▼
            
            Evict Lower Priority Pods
            
                    │
            
                    ▼
            
            Resources Become Free
            
                    │
            
                    ▼
            
            Schedule High Priority Pod
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # When Does Preemption Happen?
            
            Only after:
            
            ```text
            Filter
            
            ↓
            
            No Candidate Nodes
            ```
            
            If at least one feasible Node exists,
            
            preemption is **not** performed.
            
            ---
            
            # Important Design Principle
            
            Preemption is expensive.
            
            It causes:
            
            - Pod eviction
            - Application restart
            - Temporary disruption
            - Controller reconciliation
            
            Therefore,
            
            Kubernetes avoids preemption whenever possible.
            
            ---
            
            # Pod Priority
            
            Preemption depends entirely on:
            
            ```text
            Priority
            ```
            
            Every Pod may have an associated:
            
            ```text
            PriorityClass
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Cluster:
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            Priority
            
            1000
            
            Pod B
            
            Priority
            
            500
            
            Pod C
            
            Priority
            
            100
            ```
            
            Higher value means:
            
            ```text
            More Important
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # PriorityClass
            
            Users define priorities using:
            
            ```yaml
            apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
            kind: PriorityClass
            
            value: 100000
            ```
            
            Pods reference it using:
            
            ```yaml
            priorityClassName:
            ```
            
            The Scheduler converts that into an integer priority value during scheduling.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            ```text
            Critical System Pod
            
            Priority
            
            1,000,000
            
            ↓
            
            Production API
            
            Priority
            
            100,000
            
            ↓
            
            Batch Job
            
            Priority
            
            10
            
            ↓
            
            Testing Pod
            
            Priority
            
            1
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Scheduling Without Priority
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Node
            
            8 CPU
            ```
            
            Running:
            
            ```text
            Batch Job
            
            8 CPU
            ```
            
            New Pod:
            
            ```text
            Production API
            
            4 CPU
            ```
            
            Without priorities:
            
            ```text
            Pending Forever
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Scheduling With Priority
            
            Scheduler sees:
            
            ```text
            Production API
            
            Higher Priority
            ```
            
            Result:
            
            ```text
            Evict Batch Job
            
            ↓
            
            Free Resources
            
            ↓
            
            Schedule API
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Candidate Nodes
            
            Preemption does **not** evaluate every Node equally.
            
            First,
            
            the Scheduler identifies Nodes where preemption **might** make scheduling possible.
            
            These become:
            
            ```text
            Candidate Nodes
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Cluster:
            
            ```text
            Node A
            
            Needs
            
            2 CPU Freed
            
            ↓
            
            Possible
            
            Node B
            
            Needs
            
            20 CPU Freed
            
            ↓
            
            Impossible
            
            Node C
            
            Already Fits
            
            ↓
            
            No Preemption Needed
            ```
            
            Only Nodes where evicting lower-priority Pods could realistically make enough room are considered as preemption candidates.
            
            ---
            
            # Candidate Node Evaluation
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            All Nodes
            
            ↓
            
            Could Preemption Help?
            
            ↓
            
            Yes
            
            ↓
            
            Candidate List
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Victim Selection
            
            Once a candidate Node is found,
            
            the Scheduler chooses:
            
            ```text
            Victim Pods
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What Is a Victim?
            
            A victim is:
            
            > **A lower-priority Pod selected for eviction to free resources for a higher-priority Pod.**
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Node:
            
            ```text
            CPU
            
            8
            ```
            
            Running:
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            Priority
            
            10
            
            CPU
            
            2
            
            Pod B
            
            Priority
            
            20
            
            CPU
            
            2
            
            Pod C
            
            Priority
            
            30
            
            CPU
            
            4
            ```
            
            Incoming Pod:
            
            ```text
            Priority
            
            100
            
            Needs
            
            4 CPU
            ```
            
            Possible victim:
            
            ```text
            Pod C
            ```
            
            Resources become available.
            
            ---
            
            # Goal of Victim Selection
            
            The Scheduler attempts to:
            
            - Evict as few Pods as possible
            - Minimize disruption
            - Preserve cluster stability
            - Free sufficient resources
            
            ---
            
            # Another Example
            
            Need:
            
            ```text
            2 CPU
            ```
            
            Current Pods:
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            1 CPU
            
            Priority 5
            
            Pod B
            
            1 CPU
            
            Priority 10
            
            Pod C
            
            6 CPU
            
            Priority 50
            ```
            
            Instead of evicting:
            
            ```text
            Pod C
            ```
            
            Scheduler may evict:
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            +
            
            Pod B
            ```
            
            depending on which combination satisfies scheduling while minimizing overall impact. The exact victim selection algorithm considers multiple factors, not just CPU totals.
            
            ---
            
            # Victim Selection Strategy
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Candidate Node
            
            ↓
            
            Lower Priority Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Find Smallest Safe Set
            
            ↓
            
            Evict
            
            ↓
            
            Enough Resources?
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What Cannot Be Preempted?
            
            Pods with:
            
            ```text
            Equal Priority
            ```
            
            or
            
            ```text
            Higher Priority
            ```
            
            cannot be preempted by a lower-priority Pod.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Incoming Pod
            
            Priority
            
            100
            
            Running Pod
            
            Priority
            
            100
            ```
            
            No preemption occurs between them based solely on priority.
            
            ---
            
            # PodDisruptionBudget (PDB)
            
            Preemption should minimize application disruption.
            
            Kubernetes considers:
            
            ```text
            PodDisruptionBudget
            ```
            
            during victim selection.
            
            ---
            
            # What Is a PDB?
            
            A PodDisruptionBudget defines:
            
            > **How many Pods of an application should remain available during voluntary disruptions.**
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Application
            
            5 Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Minimum Available
            
            4
            ```
            
            Only one Pod should be disrupted at a time.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Deployment:
            
            ```text
            Web
            
            5 Pods
            ```
            
            PDB:
            
            ```yaml
            minAvailable: 4
            ```
            
            Already unavailable:
            
            ```text
            1 Pod
            ```
            
            Scheduler avoids preempting another Pod from this workload if doing so would violate the PDB.
            
            ---
            
            # Important Clarification
            
            PDBs are **best-effort** during preemption.
            
            If no alternative exists,
            
            Kubernetes may still preempt Pods in situations where scheduling a higher-priority workload is necessary.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Respect PDB?
            
            Without PDB awareness:
            
            ```text
            Preempt
            
            ↓
            
            Too Many Pods Removed
            
            ↓
            
            Application Down
            ```
            
            PDB reduces unnecessary disruption.
            
            ---
            
            # Nominated Node
            
            One of the most misunderstood concepts is:
            
            ```text
            Nominated Node
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What Is a Nominated Node?
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Scheduler
            
            ↓
            
            Chooses Node-8
            
            ↓
            
            Victims Need Time
            
            ↓
            
            Termination Begins
            ```
            
            Resources are **not** immediately available.
            
            The incoming Pod must wait.
            
            Instead of repeating the full search every moment,
            
            the Scheduler records:
            
            ```text
            Nominated Node
            
            ↓
            
            Node-8
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why?
            
            This indicates:
            
            ```text
