            # What is kubelet?
            
            The **kubelet** is the primary **Node Agent** that runs on **every Kubernetes Node**.
            
            Every Worker Node runs:
            
            ```text
            +------------------------+
            |        kubelet         |
            +------------------------+
            |   Container Runtime    |
            |        kube-proxy      |
            +------------------------+
            ```
            
            The kubelet is responsible for ensuring that every Pod assigned to its Node is running exactly as described in Kubernetes.
            
            Think of kubelet as:
            
            ```text
            Control Plane
            
            ↓
            
            Desired State
            
            ↓
            
            kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Actual Running Containers
            ```
            
            It continuously converts the desired state into the actual running state.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Does Kubernetes Need kubelet?
            
            Suppose a Deployment creates:
            
            ```yaml
            replicas: 3
            ```
            
            The Scheduler decides:
            
            ```text
            Pod-1 → worker-01
            
            Pod-2 → worker-02
            
            Pod-3 → worker-03
            ```
            
            Question:
            
            Who actually performs?
            
            - Pull image
            - Create Pod Sandbox
            - Configure networking
            - Mount volumes
            - Start containers
            - Restart crashed containers
            - Execute health probes
            - Update Pod status
            
            Answer:
            
            ```text
            kubelet
            ```
            
            Without kubelet:
            
            ```text
            Scheduler
            
            ↓
            
            Assign Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Nothing Runs
            ```
            
            The Scheduler **never** starts containers.
            
            ---
            
            # Position of kubelet in Kubernetes
            
            ```text
                             User
            
                               │
            
                       kubectl apply
            
                               │
            
                         API Server
            
                               │
            
                     Controller Manager
            
                               │
            
                         Scheduler
            
                               │
            
                   Assign Pod to Node
            
                               │
            
                      ─────────────────
            
                         Worker Node
            
                      ─────────────────
            
                           kubelet
            
                               │
            
                      Container Runtime
            
                               │
            
                         Linux Kernel
            
                               │
            
                        Running Process
            ```
            
            Notice:
            
            The kubelet exists **only on Nodes**.
            
            It is **not** part of the Control Plane.
            
            ---
            
            # kubelet Architecture
            
            Internally, kubelet consists of multiple cooperating components.
            
            High-Level Architecture:
            
            ```text
                             API Server
            
                                  ▲
            
                    Watch Pods    │    Update Status
            
                                  │
            
                            +-------------+
            
                            |   kubelet   |
            
                            +-------------+
            
                  ┌────────────┼────────────┐
            
                  ▼            ▼            ▼
            
             Pod Manager   Volume Manager  Probe Manager
            
                  │            │            │
            
                  ▼            ▼            ▼
            
             Container Runtime (CRI)
            
                  │
            
                  ▼
            
             Linux Kernel
            ```
            
            Each internal manager focuses on a specific responsibility.
            
            ---
            
            # Major Internal Components
            
            Conceptually, kubelet includes:
            
            ```text
            kubelet
            
            ├── Pod Manager
            
            ├── PodWorkers
            
            ├── Sync Loop
            
            ├── Probe Manager
            
            ├── Volume Manager
            
            ├── Secret Manager
            
            ├── ConfigMap Manager
            
            ├── Status Manager
            
            ├── Eviction Manager
            
            ├── Runtime Manager
            
            └── PLEG (Pod Lifecycle Event Generator)
            ```
            
            These components work together continuously.
            
            Later chapters explore many of them individually.
            
            ---
            
            # Primary Responsibilities of kubelet
            
            The kubelet has several major responsibilities.
            
            ---
            
            ## 1. Register the Node
            
            When kubelet starts:
            
            ```text
            Collect Node Information
            
            ↓
            
            Register Node
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            ```
            
            Without registration:
            
            ```text
            Node
            
            ↓
            
            Unknown
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler Cannot Use It
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ## 2. Watch Assigned Pods
            
            The kubelet watches the API Server.
            
            It receives only Pods assigned to its own Node.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            worker-01
            
            ↓
            
            Watch:
            
            nodeName=worker-01
            ```
            
            It ignores Pods scheduled elsewhere.
            
            ---
            
            ## 3. Run Containers
            
            The kubelet asks the Container Runtime to:
            
            ```text
            Pull Image
            
            ↓
            
            Create Sandbox
            
            ↓
            
            Create Container
            
            ↓
            
            Start Container
            ```
            
            The kubelet never runs containers directly.
            
            It delegates execution through the **Container Runtime Interface (CRI)**.
            
            ---
            
            ## 4. Monitor Containers
            
            The kubelet continuously checks:
            
            - Running state
            - Exit codes
            - Restart policy
            - Health probes
            - Resource usage
            
            It keeps comparing:
            
            ```text
            Desired State
            
            ↓
            
            Actual State
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ## 5. Update Pod Status
            
            The kubelet reports:
            
            ```text
            Pending
            
            Running
            
            Succeeded
            
            Failed
            ```
            
            back to the API Server.
            
            ---
            
            ## 6. Update Node Health
            
            The kubelet periodically reports:
            
            ```text
            Ready
            
            MemoryPressure
            
            DiskPressure
            
            PIDPressure
            ```
            
            This information is stored in the Node object.
            
            ---
            
            ## 7. Execute Health Probes
            
            The kubelet runs:
            
            ```text
            Readiness
            
            Liveness
            
            Startup
            ```
            
            Probes are executed locally on the Node.
            
            No Control Plane component performs them.
            
            ---
            
            ## 8. Handle Graceful Shutdown
            
            When a Pod is deleted:
            
            ```text
            SIGTERM
            
            ↓
            
            Grace Period
            
            ↓
            
            SIGKILL (if necessary)
            ```
            
            The kubelet coordinates the shutdown.
            
            ---
            
            ## 9. Evict Pods During Resource Pressure
            
            If the Node experiences:
            
            ```text
            Memory Pressure
            
            Disk Pressure
            
            PID Pressure
            ```
            
            the kubelet may evict Pods to keep the Node healthy.
            
            ---
            
            # What kubelet Does NOT Do
            
            A common interview topic.
            
            The kubelet does **not**:
            
            ❌ Schedule Pods
            
            ❌ Create Deployments
            
            ❌ Create ReplicaSets
            
            ❌ Store cluster state
            
            ❌ Perform leader election
            
            ❌ Manage etcd
            
            Those are responsibilities of other control plane components.
            
            ---
            
            # kubelet Communication
            
            The kubelet communicates with several components.
            
            ```text
                             kubelet
            
                  ┌────────────┼────────────┐
            
                  ▼            ▼            ▼
            
             API Server     CRI Runtime     CSI
            
                  │                           │
            
                  ▼                           ▼
            
               Cluster State             Storage
            
                  ▲
            
                  │
            
                  ▼
            
                 CNI
            ```
            
            Notice:
            
            The kubelet is the integration point for many Kubernetes subsystems.
            
            ---
            
            # kubelet Startup Sequence
            
            When kubelet starts, several initialization steps occur before it begins managing Pods.
            
            ---
            
            ## Step 1 — Load Configuration
            
            The kubelet reads:
            
            ```text
            Configuration File
            
            ↓
            
            Command Line Flags
            
            ↓
            
            Environment
            ```
            
            Examples include:
            
            - Node name
            - Runtime endpoint
            - Authentication settings
            - Certificates
            - Eviction thresholds
            
            ---
            
            ## Step 2 — Initialize Internal Managers
            
            The kubelet creates:
            
            ```text
            Pod Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Volume Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Probe Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Status Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Runtime Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Eviction Manager
            ```
            
            These managers remain active for the lifetime of kubelet.
            
            ---
            
            ## Step 3 — Connect to Container Runtime
            
            Using CRI:
            
            ```text
            kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            gRPC
            
            ↓
            
            containerd
            
            or
            
            CRI-O
            ```
            
            If the runtime cannot be reached:
            
            ```text
            No Runtime
            
            ↓
            
            No Pods Can Start
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ## Step 4 — Discover Local State
            
            The kubelet examines:
            
            - Existing containers
            - Existing sandboxes
            - Mounted volumes
            - Static Pod manifests
            
            This enables recovery after restarts.
            
            ---
            
            ## Step 5 — Register the Node
            
            If registration is enabled:
            
            ```text
            Gather Node Information
            
            ↓
            
            Create Node Object
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            ```
            
            The Scheduler can now see the Node.
            
            ---
            
            ## Step 6 — Start Watches
            
            The kubelet begins watching:
            
            ```text
            Assigned Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Secrets
            
            ↓
            
            ConfigMaps
            ```
            
            Changes are delivered as watch events.
            
            ---
            
            ## Step 7 — Start Sync Loop
            
            Finally:
            
            ```text
            Pod Sync Loop
            
            ↓
            
            Reconcile Desired State
            
            ↓
            
            Manage Pods
            ```
            
            At this point the Node becomes operational.
            
            ---
            
            # Startup Sequence Diagram
            
            ```text
            kubelet Starts
            
            ↓
            
            Load Configuration
            
            ↓
            
            Initialize Managers
            
            ↓
            
            Connect CRI
            
            ↓
            
            Discover Local State
            
            ↓
            
            Register Node
            
            ↓
            
            Start Watches
            
            ↓
            
            Start Pod Sync Loop
            
            ↓
            
            Node Ready
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Node Registration
            
            Every Worker Node must appear inside Kubernetes as a Node object.
            
            The kubelet creates or updates this object.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Register?
            
            Without registration:
            
            ```text
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            No Node Object
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler Cannot Place Pods
            ```
            
            The Node effectively does not exist from Kubernetes' perspective.
            
            ---
            
            # Registration Workflow
            
            ```text
            kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Collect System Information
            
            ↓
            
            Create Node Object
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            etcd
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Information Sent During Registration
            
            The kubelet reports information such as:
            
            ```text
            Node Name
            
            Architecture
            
            Operating System
            
            Kernel Version
            
            Container Runtime
            
            CPU Capacity
            
            Memory Capacity
            
            Ephemeral Storage
            
            Internal IP
            
            Hostname
            
            Labels
            
            Taints
            ```
            
            This becomes part of the Node object.
            
            ---
            
            # Node Registration Example
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```yaml
            Node
            
            metadata:
            
              name: worker-01
            
            status:
            
              capacity:
            
                cpu: 16
            
                memory: 64Gi
            
              nodeInfo:
            
                kernelVersion: ...
            
                containerRuntimeVersion: ...
            ```
            
            The Scheduler later uses this information.
            
            ---
            
            # Registration Architecture
            
            ```text
            Worker Node
            
            ↓
            
            kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Node Object
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            etcd
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler Can See Node
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Node Status Updates
            
            Registration occurs once,
            
            but Node status is updated continuously.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Update Status?
            
            Node conditions constantly change.
            
            Examples:
            
            ```text
            Disk Usage
            
            Memory Usage
            
            Network Availability
            
            Runtime Health
            ```
            
            The Control Plane must know the current health.
            
            ---
            
            # Node Conditions
            
            The kubelet updates conditions such as:
            
            ```text
            Ready
            
            MemoryPressure
            
            DiskPressure
            
            PIDPressure
            
            NetworkUnavailable
            ```
            
            Each condition is either:
            
            ```text
            True
            
            False
            
            Unknown
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Ready Condition
            
            The most important condition is:
            
            ```text
            Ready
            ```
            
            Meaning:
            
            ```text
            Can This Node
            
            Run Pods?
            ```
            
            If Ready becomes:
            
            ```text
            False
            
            or
            
            Unknown
            ```
            
            the Node Controller eventually begins handling the failure.
            
            ---
            
            # Status Update Workflow
            
            ```text
            Node Metrics
            
            ↓
            
            Evaluate Conditions
            
            ↓
            
            Update Node Status
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            etcd
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Node Lease (Heartbeat)
            
            Updating the full Node object frequently is expensive.
            
            Modern Kubernetes primarily uses:
            
            ```text
            Node Lease
            ```
            
            for heartbeats.
            
            ---
            
            # What is a Node Lease?
            
            A Lease is a lightweight object in:
            
            ```text
            coordination.k8s.io
            ```
            
            It contains essentially:
            
            ```text
            Node Name
            
            Renew Time
            ```
            
            Nothing more.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Use Node Leases?
            
            Without Leases:
            
            ```text
            Every Heartbeat
            
            ↓
            
            Update Entire Node Object
            
            ↓
            
            Large API Traffic
            
            ↓
            
            Higher etcd Load
            ```
            
            With Leases:
            
            ```text
            Heartbeat
            
            ↓
            
            Tiny Lease Update
            
            ↓
            
            Very Low Overhead
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Heartbeat Workflow
            
            ```text
            kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Renew Lease
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            Lease Updated
            
            ↓
            
            Node Controller Watches Lease
            ```
            
            If the Lease stops updating:
            
            ```text
            Node Controller
            
            ↓
            
            Heartbeat Missing
            
            ↓
            
            Node May Be Unreachable
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Node Lease vs Node Status
            
            These are often confused.
            
            | Node Status | Node Lease |
            |-------------|------------|
            | Full health information | Lightweight heartbeat |
            | Contains Conditions | Contains renewal timestamp |
            | Larger object | Very small object |
            | Updated less frequently | Updated frequently |
            | Used for Node health details | Used for liveness detection |
            
            Both work together.
            
            ---
            
            # Complete Node Registration Workflow
            
            ```text
            kubelet Starts
            
            ↓
            
            Load Configuration
            
            ↓
            
            Connect Runtime
            
            ↓
            
            Collect Node Information
            
            ↓
            
            Create Node Object
            
            ↓
            
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            etcd
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler Discovers Node
            
            ↓
            
            Start Lease Updates
            
            ↓
            
            Start Status Updates
            
            ↓
            
            Begin Managing Pods
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Production Example 1 — New Worker Joins
            
            ```text
            New VM
            
            ↓
            
            Install kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Start kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Register Node
            
            ↓
            
            Node Appears
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler Uses Node
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Production Example 2 — Runtime Failure
            
            ```text
            containerd Stops
            
            ↓
            
            kubelet Detects Runtime Failure
            
            ↓
            
            Ready=False
            
            ↓
            
            Node Controller Observes
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler Avoids Node
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Production Example 3 — Network Partition
            
            ```text
            Worker Node
            
            ↓
            
            Cannot Reach API Server
            
            ↓
            
            Lease Stops Updating
            
            ↓
            
            Node Controller
            
            ↓
            
            Node Becomes NotReady
            ```
            
            Existing Pods may continue running locally, but the Control Plane cannot communicate with the Node.
            
            ---
            
            # Production Example 4 — Node Recovery
            
            ```text
            Network Restored
            
            ↓
            
            Lease Renewed
            
            ↓
            
            Status Updated
            
            ↓
            
            Ready=True
            
            ↓
            
            Scheduler Can Use Node Again
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Production Best Practices
            
            ✔ Always enable Node Lease (default in modern Kubernetes).
            
            ✔ Monitor kubelet health and logs.
            
            ✔ Use TLS authentication between kubelet and API Server.
            
            ✔ Ensure accurate Node labels and taints before scheduling workloads.
            
            ✔ Monitor Node Conditions for early detection of hardware or runtime issues.
            
            ✔ Keep kubelet and the container runtime version-compatible.
            
            ✔ Avoid unnecessary kubelet restarts in production.
            
