            # Why Node Pressure Exists
            
            A Kubernetes Node has finite resources.
            
            ```text
            CPU
            
            Memory
            
            Disk
            
            PIDs
            ```
            
            If these resources become exhausted, the entire Node may fail.
            
            Consider this situation:
            
            ```text
            Node Memory
            
            64 GB
            
            ↓
            
            Applications Allocate Memory
            
            ↓
            
            63 GB
            
            ↓
            
            63.8 GB
            
            ↓
            
            63.95 GB
            
            ↓
            
            64 GB
            ```
            
            Eventually:
            
            ```text
            Linux
            
            ↓
            
            Out Of Memory
            
            ↓
            
            OOM Killer
            
            ↓
            
            Random Processes Killed
            ```
            
            This may include:
            
            - kubelet
            - container runtime
            - system services
            - application containers
            
            Instead of allowing the operating system to fail unpredictably, Kubernetes proactively evicts Pods.
            
            ---
            
            # What is Node Pressure?
            
            **Node Pressure** occurs when a Node experiences resource shortages severe enough that kubelet must take action.
            
            Examples include:
            
            ```text
            Memory Pressure
            
            Disk Pressure
            
            PID Pressure
            ```
            
            These conditions indicate that the Node cannot safely continue operating without intervention.
            
            ---
            
            # High-Level Workflow
            
            ```text
            Applications
            
            ↓
            
            Consume Resources
            
            ↓
            
            Node Pressure
            
            ↓
            
            Eviction Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Select Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Gracefully Evict
            
            ↓
            
            Node Healthy Again
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why Doesn't Linux Handle Everything?
            
            Linux has mechanisms like:
            
            ```text
            OOM Killer
            ```
            
            However, the Linux kernel:
            
            - Does not understand Kubernetes workloads.
            - Does not understand Pod priorities.
            - Does not understand QoS classes.
            
            Linux simply kills processes.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Random Process
            
            ↓
            
            Killed
            
            ↓
            
            Critical Application Lost
            ```
            
            Kubernetes provides smarter workload-aware decisions.
            
            ---
            
            # Kubernetes vs Linux
            
            Linux:
            
            ```text
            Memory Exhausted
            
            ↓
            
            OOM Killer
            
            ↓
            
            Random Victim
            ```
            
            Kubernetes:
            
            ```text
            Memory Exhausted
            
            ↓
            
            Eviction Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Select Best Candidate
            
            ↓
            
            Graceful Eviction
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Types of Node Pressure
            
            Kubernetes currently recognizes several major pressure conditions.
            
            ---
            
            ## Memory Pressure
            
            Occurs when available memory becomes critically low.
            
            ```text
            RAM
            
            ↓
            
            Almost Full
            
            ↓
            
            MemoryPressure=True
            ```
            
            Memory is the most common cause of Pod evictions.
            
            ---
            
            ## Disk Pressure
            
            Occurs when storage becomes critically full.
            
            Examples:
            
            ```text
            Container Images
            
            Logs
            
            Volumes
            
            EmptyDir
            
            Writable Layers
            ```
            
            If storage fills completely:
            
            ```text
            New Pods Cannot Start
            
            Logs Cannot Be Written
            
            Images Cannot Be Pulled
            ```
            
            ---
            
            ## PID Pressure
            
            Linux limits the number of processes (PIDs).
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Maximum PIDs
            
            32768
            
            ↓
            
            32760 Used
            
            ↓
            
            32767 Used
            
            ↓
            
            No New Processes
            ```
            
            Containers can no longer create processes.
            
            ---
            
            # Node Conditions
            
            The kubelet reports these conditions through the Node object.
            
            ```text
            Ready
            
            MemoryPressure
            
            DiskPressure
            
            PIDPressure
            ```
            
            Each condition is continuously updated.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            MemoryPressure
            
            ↓
            
            True
            ```
            
            The Scheduler avoids placing new Pods on a Node experiencing pressure.
            
            ---
            
            # Eviction Signals
            
            The Eviction Manager continuously monitors resource usage.
            
            Examples of signals include:
            
            ```text
            Available Memory
            
            Available Disk Space
            
            Available Inodes
            
            Available PIDs
            ```
            
            These signals are compared against configured thresholds.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Memory Available
            
            ↓
            
            10%
            
            ↓
            
            8%
            
            ↓
            
            5%
            
            ↓
            
            3%
            ```
            
            Eventually:
            
            ```text
            Threshold Crossed
            
            ↓
            
            Eviction Begins
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Eviction Thresholds
            
            The kubelet supports configurable eviction thresholds.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Available Memory
            
            < Threshold
            
            ↓
            
            Evict Pods
            ```
            
            Thresholds may be configured as:
            
            ```text
            Hard
            
            or
            
            Soft
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Hard Eviction Threshold
            
            Hard thresholds require immediate action.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Available Memory
            
            ↓
            
            Critical
            
            ↓
            
            Immediate Eviction
            ```
            
            The kubelet begins evicting Pods without waiting for recovery.
            
            ---
            
            # Soft Eviction Threshold
            
            Soft thresholds allow temporary resource shortages.
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            Pressure Detected
            
            ↓
            
            Grace Period
            
            ↓
            
            Still Under Pressure?
            
            │
            
            ├── No
            
            │
            
            ▼
            
            Continue Running
            
            │
            
            └── Yes
            
                 │
            
                 ▼
            
            Eviction
            ```
            
            This prevents unnecessary evictions caused by brief resource spikes.
            
            ---
            
            # Eviction Manager
            
            The **Eviction Manager** is an internal kubelet component responsible for protecting Node stability.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Resource Monitor
            
            ↓
            
            Eviction Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Select Victim
            
            ↓
            
            Evict Pod
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Responsibilities of Eviction Manager
            
            The Eviction Manager:
            
            - Monitors Node resources
            - Evaluates eviction thresholds
            - Detects Node pressure
            - Chooses Pods for eviction
            - Performs graceful eviction
            - Updates Pod status
            
            ---
            
            # Internal Architecture
            
            ```text
            Node Metrics
            
            ↓
            
            Eviction Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Threshold Crossed?
            
            │
            
            ├── No
            
            │
            
            ▼
            
            Continue Monitoring
            
            │
            
            └── Yes
            
                 │
            
                 ▼
            
            Select Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Graceful Eviction
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Node Pressure Eviction
            
            Eviction is different from deletion.
            
            Deletion:
            
            ```text
            User
            
            ↓
            
            kubectl delete
            ```
            
            Eviction:
            
            ```text
            Node Resource Pressure
            
            ↓
            
            kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Protect Node
            ```
            
            Evictions are automatic.
            
            ---
            
            # Node Pressure Eviction Workflow
            
            ```text
            Low Memory
            
            ↓
            
            MemoryPressure=True
            
            ↓
            
            Eviction Manager
            
            ↓
            
            Choose Victim
            
            ↓
            
            Terminate Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Free Resources
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Pod Selection for Eviction
            
            Not every Pod has equal importance.
            
            The Eviction Manager evaluates several factors before selecting Pods.
            
            ---
            
            # Primary Selection Criteria
            
            Generally, the kubelet prefers to evict Pods that:
            
            - Exceed their resource requests
            - Have lower QoS
            - Have lower priority
            - Help relieve the current resource pressure
            
            The goal is to free sufficient resources while minimizing impact on critical workloads.
            
            ---
            
            # Resource Requests Matter
            
            Consider two Pods.
            
            Pod A:
            
            ```text
            Request
            
            1 GB
            
            Usage
            
            900 MB
            ```
            
            Pod B:
            
            ```text
            Request
            
            1 GB
            
            Usage
            
            3 GB
            ```
            
            Pod B is consuming significantly more memory than requested and is more likely to be selected.
            
            ---
            
            # Pod Priority
            
            Higher-priority Pods receive stronger protection.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Critical DNS
            
            Priority
            
            1,000,000
            
            ────────────
            
            Test Application
            
            Priority
            
            0
            ```
            
            The test application is a more likely eviction candidate.
            
            ---
            
            # QoS Classes
            
            Every Pod belongs to one of three Quality of Service (QoS) classes.
            
            ```text
            Guaranteed
            
            Burstable
            
            BestEffort
            ```
            
            QoS influences eviction order.
            
            ---
            
            # Guaranteed
            
            Requirements:
            
            ```text
            Requests
            
            =
            
            Limits
            
            For Every Container
            ```
            
            Example:
            
            ```yaml
            requests:
            
              memory: 2Gi
            
            limits:
            
              memory: 2Gi
            ```
            
            These Pods receive the strongest protection.
            
            ---
            
            # Burstable
            
            Requests are specified,
            
            but requests and limits differ.
            
            Example:
            
            ```yaml
            requests:
            
              memory: 1Gi
            
            limits:
            
              memory: 4Gi
            ```
            
            Burstable Pods receive medium protection.
            
            ---
            
            # BestEffort
            
            No CPU or memory requests or limits are defined.
            
            Example:
            
            ```yaml
            resources: {}
            ```
            
            These Pods receive the lowest protection.
            
            ---
            
            # QoS Eviction Order
            
            Under memory pressure, Kubernetes generally prefers this order:
            
            ```text
            BestEffort
            
            ↓
            
            Burstable
            
            ↓
            
            Guaranteed
            ```
            
            This is a **general priority**, not an absolute rule. Other factors such as actual resource usage, requests, and Pod Priority are also considered.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Node Memory:
            
            ```text
            64 GB
            ```
            
            Pods:
            
            ```text
            BestEffort
            
            4 GB
            
            ────────────
            
            Burstable
            
            10 GB
            
            ────────────
            
            Guaranteed
            
            20 GB
            ```
            
            The BestEffort Pod is generally the first eviction candidate.
            
            ---
            
            # Eviction Timeline
            
            ```text
            Memory Usage
            
            ↓
            
            Threshold Crossed
            
            ↓
            
            Eviction Manager
            
            ↓
            
            BestEffort Removed
            
            ↓
            
            Memory Still Low?
            
            │
            
            ├── No
            
            │
            
            ▼
            
            Done
            
            │
            
            └── Yes
            
                 │
            
                 ▼
            
            Burstable Removed
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Graceful Termination
            
            Eviction does not immediately kill containers.
            
            Instead, kubelet performs graceful shutdown.
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            Eviction
            
            ↓
            
            SIGTERM
            
            ↓
            
            Grace Period
            
            ↓
            
            Container Exits
            
            ↓
            
            SIGKILL
            
            (If Necessary)
            ```
            
            Applications receive an opportunity to clean up.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Graceful Termination Matters
            
            Applications may need to:
            
            - Finish requests
            - Flush logs
            - Commit transactions
            - Close database connections
            
            Without graceful shutdown:
            
            ```text
            Application
            
            ↓
            
            Killed Immediately
            
            ↓
            
            Data Loss
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Grace Period
            
            Each Pod may specify:
            
            ```text
            terminationGracePeriodSeconds
            ```
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            30 Seconds
            ```
            
            Timeline:
            
            ```text
            SIGTERM
            
            ↓
            
            Application Shutdown
            
            ↓
            
            Exit
            
            ↓
            
            Complete
            ```
            
            If the process is still running after the grace period:
            
            ```text
            SIGKILL
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Graceful Termination Workflow
            
            ```text
            Eviction
            
            ↓
            
            Mark Pod Terminating
            
            ↓
            
            Run preStop Hook (if configured)
            
            ↓
            
            Send SIGTERM
            
            ↓
            
            Wait
            
            ↓
            
            Exited?
            
            │
            
            ├── Yes
            
            │
            
            ▼
            
            Delete Pod
            
            │
            
            └── No
            
                 │
            
                 ▼
            
            SIGKILL
            
            ↓
            
            Delete Pod
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Graceful Node Shutdown
            
            Modern kubelet versions support **Graceful Node Shutdown**.
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Machine Shutdown
            
            ↓
            
            Power Off
            ```
            
            Without coordination:
            
            ```text
            Containers
            
            ↓
            
            Killed Immediately
            ```
            
            Instead:
            
            ```text
            Shutdown Event
            
            ↓
            
            kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Gracefully Terminate Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Operating System Shutdown
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Shutdown Workflow
            
            ```text
            Operating System
            
            ↓
            
            Shutdown Signal
            
            ↓
            
            kubelet
            
            ↓
            
            Evict Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Graceful Termination
            
            ↓
            
            Node Power Off
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Priority During Shutdown
            
            During shutdown, kubelet can terminate Pods in stages.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Normal Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Critical Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Shutdown Complete
            ```
            
            Critical system Pods are given additional time where configured.
            
            ---
            
            # Difference Between Eviction and Deletion
            
            | Eviction | Deletion |
            |-----------|----------|
            | Triggered automatically | Triggered by a user or controller |
            | Caused by Node pressure | Caused by API operations |
            | Protects Node health | Removes workload intentionally |
            | Managed by Eviction Manager | Managed through Kubernetes API |
            | Includes graceful termination | Also supports graceful termination |
            
