            # Where This Chapter Fits
            
            So far we have learned:
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            ClusterIP
            
            ↓
            
            kube-proxy
            
            ↓
            
            iptables/IPVS
            
            ↓
            
            Pod
            ```
            
            But we still haven't answered one important question:
            
            > **How does Kubernetes decide which Pod should receive the request?**
            
            Suppose we have:
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            ────────────
            
            Pod B
            
            ────────────
            
            Pod C
            ```
            
            Who receives the next request?
            
            This chapter answers that question.
            
            ---
            
            # The Problem
            
            Suppose a Deployment has:
            
            ```text
            10 Pods
            ```
            
            Every Pod exposes:
            
            ```text
            Port 8080
            ```
            
            Clients connect to:
            
            ```text
            ClusterIP
            
            10.96.0.15
            ```
            
            The challenge is:
            
            ```text
            One Service
            
            ↓
            
            Many Pods
            ```
            
            Traffic must be distributed correctly.
            
            ---
            
            # Kubernetes Solution
            
            Instead of exposing Pods directly,
            
            Kubernetes creates a Service.
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Service
            
            ↓
            
            Backend Pods
            ```
            
            The Service becomes the stable entry point.
            
            ---
            
            # High-Level Service Architecture
            
            ```text
                             Client
            
                                │
            
                                ▼
            
                          ClusterIP Service
            
                                │
            
                                ▼
            
                           kube-proxy
            
                                │
            
                      Select Backend Pod
            
                                │
            
                  ┌────────┼────────┐
            
                  ▼        ▼        ▼
            
                Pod A    Pod B    Pod C
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Important Principle
            
            A Kubernetes Service does **not** contain Pods.
            
            Instead,
            
            it contains:
            
            ```text
            Label Selector
            ```
            
            The selector determines which Pods belong to the Service.
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Service:
            
            ```yaml
            selector:
              app: payment
            ```
            
            Matching Pods:
            
            ```text
            payment-1
            
            payment-2
            
            payment-3
            ```
            
            The Service automatically includes matching Pods.
            
            ---
            
            # Backend Discovery
            
            How does Kubernetes discover Pods?
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            Service Selector
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlice Controller
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlices
            
            ↓
            
            kube-proxy
            ```
            
            Notice:
            
            The Service never directly stores Pod IPs.
            
            ---
            
            # Service Load Balancing Architecture
            
            Complete architecture:
            
            ```text
                        Deployment
            
                              │
            
                          Creates Pods
            
                              │
            
                              ▼
            
                          EndpointSlice
            
                              │
            
                              ▼
            
                           kube-proxy
            
                              │
            
                    iptables / IPVS / eBPF
            
                              │
            
                              ▼
            
                         Backend Pod
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # ClusterIP Load Balancing
            
            ClusterIP is the default Service type.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Service
            
            10.96.0.15
            ```
            
            Clients always connect to:
            
            ```text
            10.96.0.15
            ```
            
            Never directly to Pods.
            
            ---
            
            # ClusterIP Workflow
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            ClusterIP
            
            ↓
            
            Backend Selection
            
            ↓
            
            Pod
            ```
            
            The client never knows which Pod handled the request.
            
            ---
            
            # Multiple Requests
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Request 1
            
            ↓
            
            Pod A
            
            ────────────
            
            Request 2
            
            ↓
            
            Pod C
            
            ────────────
            
            Request 3
            
            ↓
            
            Pod B
            ```
            
            Traffic is distributed according to the dataplane's load-balancing behavior and connection state.
            
            ---
            
            # Endpoint Selection
            
            A Service may have many endpoints.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            10.244.1.5
            
            ────────────
            
            Pod B
            
            10.244.2.8
            
            ────────────
            
            Pod C
            
            10.244.3.9
            ```
            
            The dataplane selects one backend for a new connection.
            
            ---
            
            # Selection Workflow
            
            ```text
            ClusterIP
            
            ↓
            
            Endpoint List
            
            ↓
            
            Choose Backend
            
            ↓
            
            Forward Request
            ```
            
            The exact selection algorithm depends on the dataplane (iptables, IPVS, or eBPF).
            
            ---
            
            # Where Do Endpoints Come From?
            
            Not from kube-proxy.
            
            Instead:
            
            ```text
            Pods
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlice Controller
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlices
            
            ↓
            
            kube-proxy
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # EndpointSlices
            
            Earlier Kubernetes versions used:
            
            ```text
            Endpoints
            ```
            
            Modern Kubernetes uses:
            
            ```text
            EndpointSlices
            ```
            
            EndpointSlices improve scalability.
            
            ---
            
            # Why EndpointSlices?
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            1,000 Pods
            ```
            
            Putting all endpoints into one object becomes inefficient.
            
            Instead:
            
            ```text
            EndpointSlice 1
            
            100 Endpoints
            
            ────────────
            
            EndpointSlice 2
            
            100 Endpoints
            
            ────────────
            
            EndpointSlice 3
            
            100 Endpoints
            ```
            
            Each EndpointSlice typically contains up to around 100 endpoints by default, though this limit is configurable.
            
            ---
            
            # EndpointSlice Architecture
            
            ```text
            Deployment
            
            ↓
            
            Pods
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlice Controller
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlices
            
            ↓
            
            kube-proxy
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # EndpointSlice Contents
            
            Each endpoint includes information such as:
            
            - Pod IP
            - Port
            - Protocol
            - Conditions (Ready, Serving, Terminating)
            - Node name
            - Zone (when available)
            
            This allows intelligent routing decisions.
            
            ---
            
            # Ready Endpoints
            
            Not every Pod receives traffic.
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            Ready
            
            ────────────
            
            Pod B
            
            Ready
            
            ────────────
            
            Pod C
            
            Not Ready
            ```
            
            Only Ready endpoints are normally included for Service traffic.
            
            ---
            
            # Why?
            
            Traffic should never reach a Pod that cannot serve requests.
            
            Readiness determines whether a Pod participates in load balancing.
            
            ---
            
            # Session Affinity
            
            Normally,
            
            every new connection may reach a different backend.
            
            Sometimes,
            
            this is undesirable.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Shopping Cart
            
            ↓
            
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Same Backend
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What is Session Affinity?
            
            Session Affinity attempts to send requests from the same client to the same backend.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Client A
            
            ↓
            
            Pod B
            
            ────────────
            
            Client A
            
            ↓
            
            Pod B
            
            Again
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why Use Session Affinity?
            
            Useful for:
            
            - Legacy applications
            - In-memory sessions
            - Stateful applications
            - Cache locality
            
            ---
            
            # ClientIP Affinity
            
            Kubernetes currently supports:
            
            ```text
            ClientIP
            ```
            
            Requests from the same client IP are directed to the same backend for the configured affinity timeout period, provided that backend remains eligible.
            
            ---
            
            # ClientIP Workflow
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Remember Backend
            
            ↓
            
            Future Requests
            
            ↓
            
            Same Pod
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Important Note
            
            Session Affinity is **not** the same as TCP connection tracking.
            
            Connection tracking ensures packets in a connection stay together.
            
            Session Affinity attempts to keep **multiple connections** from the same client directed to the same backend.
            
            ---
            
            # conntrack vs Session Affinity
            
            | conntrack | Session Affinity |
            |------------|------------------|
            | Same TCP connection | Multiple connections |
            | Kernel feature | Kubernetes Service feature |
            | Always required | Optional |
            | Packet consistency | Client stickiness |
            
            ---
            
            # InternalTrafficPolicy
            
            Sometimes traffic should remain:
            
            ```text
            Inside Same Node
            ```
            
            This is controlled by:
            
            ```text
            InternalTrafficPolicy
            ```
            
            Possible values:
            
            ```text
            Cluster
            
            Local
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Cluster Policy
            
            ```text
            Node A
            
            ↓
            
            Pod Anywhere
            ```
            
            Traffic may be forwarded to Pods on any Node.
            
            ---
            
            # Local Policy
            
            ```text
            Node A
            
            ↓
            
            Only Local Pods
            ```
            
            If no local endpoints exist,
            
            the Service has no eligible backend for traffic originating from that Node.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Use Local?
            
            Advantages:
            
            - Lower latency
            - Reduced cross-node traffic
            - Better network locality
            
            Trade-off:
            
            Potentially uneven traffic distribution if endpoint placement is imbalanced.
            
            ---
            
            # ExternalTrafficPolicy
            
            This policy applies to traffic entering the cluster through:
            
            - NodePort
            - LoadBalancer Services
            
            Possible values:
            
            ```text
            Cluster
            
            Local
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # ExternalTrafficPolicy = Cluster
            
            Traffic may arrive at:
            
            ```text
            Node A
            ```
            
            and be forwarded to:
            
            ```text
            Pod on Node B
            ```
            
            This maximizes backend availability.
            
            ---
            
            # ExternalTrafficPolicy = Local
            
            Traffic entering:
            
            ```text
            Node A
            ```
            
            can only be forwarded to Pods on:
            
            ```text
            Node A
            ```
            
            If no local endpoints exist,
            
            that Node does not serve external traffic for the Service.
            
            ---
            
            # Why Use Local?
            
            One major advantage:
            
            ```text
            Preserve Client IP
            ```
            
            Because unnecessary cross-node forwarding and SNAT can often be avoided.
            
            Trade-offs include reduced load distribution flexibility if some Nodes have no local endpoints.
            
            ---
            
            # Health Checking
            
            Kubernetes should not send traffic to unhealthy Pods.
            
            How does it know?
            
            Through:
            
            ```text
            Readiness Probe
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Workflow
            
            ```text
            Readiness Probe
            
            ↓
            
            Ready?
            
            ↓
            
            Yes
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlice
            
            ↓
            
            Traffic
            
            ────────────
            
            No
            
            ↓
            
            Remove Endpoint
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Health Check Example
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            Ready
            
            ────────────
            
            Pod B
            
            Ready
            
            ────────────
            
            Pod C
            
            Failed Readiness
            ```
            
            Traffic only reaches:
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            Pod B
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Rolling Updates
            
            Suppose:
            
            Version 1
            
            ```text
            Pod A
            
            Pod B
            
            Pod C
            ```
            
            New deployment begins.
            
            ---
            
            # Update Process
            
            ```text
            New Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Ready
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlice Updated
            
            ↓
            
            Traffic Starts
            
            ↓
            
            Old Pod Removed
            
            ↓
            
            Traffic Stops
            ```
            
            No downtime occurs when the rollout is configured correctly.
            
            ---
            
            # Rolling Update Timeline
            
            ```text
            Old Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Running
            
            ────────────
            
            New Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Created
            
            ↓
            
            Ready
            
            ↓
            
            Traffic Enabled
            
            ↓
            
            Old Pod Deleted
            ```
            
            Traffic shifts only after readiness is confirmed.
            
            ---
            
            # Scaling Events
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            3 Pods
            ```
            
            Autoscaler increases to:
            
            ```text
            10 Pods
            ```
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            New Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Ready
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlices Updated
            
            ↓
            
            kube-proxy
            
            ↓
            
            Traffic Distributed
            ```
            
            No Service restart occurs.
            
            ---
            
            # Scale Down
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            10 Pods
            
            ↓
            
            3 Pods
            ```
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            Old Pods
            
            ↓
            
            Termination
            
            ↓
            
            Endpoint Removed
            
            ↓
            
            Traffic Stops
            ```
            
            The endpoint is removed before the Pod is terminated to minimize request failures.
            
            ---
            
            # Failure Handling
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Pod B
            
            ↓
            
            Crash
            ```
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            Readiness Fails
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlice Updated
            
            ↓
            
            Remove Endpoint
            
            ↓
            
            Traffic
            
            ↓
            
            Healthy Pods
            ```
            
            No manual intervention is required.
            
            ---
            
            # Node Failure
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Entire Node
            
            ↓
            
            Offline
            ```
            
            Workflow:
            
            ```text
            Node Controller
            
            ↓
            
            Pods Lost
            
            ↓
            
            EndpointSlice Updated
            
            ↓
            
            Traffic Redirected
            ```
            
            Clients continue using the same Service IP.
            
