            ---
            
            # Why Replace kube-proxy?
            
            Traditional kube-proxy works well.
            
            But as clusters grow,
            
            certain limitations become more visible.
            
            Examples:
            
            ```text
            Large iptables Rule Sets
            
            ────────────
            
            Rule Synchronization
            
            ────────────
            
            Complex Debugging
            
            ────────────
            
            Limited Visibility
            ```
            
            Modern platforms wanted a faster and more programmable dataplane.
            
            ---
            
            # Evolution of Kubernetes Networking
            
            ```text
            Userspace Proxy
            
            ↓
            
            iptables
            
            ↓
            
            IPVS
            
            ↓
            
            eBPF
            ```
            
            Each generation moved more packet processing into efficient kernel components.
            
            ---
            
            # What is eBPF?
            
            eBPF stands for:
            
            ```text
            Extended Berkeley Packet Filter
            ```
            
            Originally,
            
            BPF was designed for packet filtering.
            
            Modern eBPF has evolved into a general-purpose programmable execution environment inside the Linux kernel.
            
            ---
            
            # Important Definition
            
            eBPF is **not** a networking tool.
            
            It is a kernel technology that allows verified programs to run safely inside the Linux kernel.
            
            Networking is only one of its many use cases.
            
            ---
            
            # Traditional Networking
            
            Without eBPF:
            
            ```text
            Packet
            
            ↓
            
            Kernel
            
            ↓
            
            iptables Rules
            
            ↓
            
            Forward
            ```
            
            The kernel follows predefined networking logic.
            
            ---
            
            # eBPF Networking
            
            With eBPF:
            
            ```text
            Packet
            
            ↓
            
            eBPF Program
            
            ↓
            
            Decision
            
            ↓
            
            Forward
            ```
            
            The kernel executes custom verified logic at specific hook points.
            
            ---
            
            # Why is eBPF Safe?
            
            Running arbitrary code in the kernel would be dangerous.
            
            Before execution,
            
            every eBPF program passes through the:
            
            ```text
            Verifier
            ```
            
            The verifier checks:
            
            - Program safety
            - Memory safety
            - Loop correctness
            - Execution limits
            
            Only verified programs are loaded.
            
            ---
            
            # eBPF Architecture
            
            ```text
                            Userspace
            
                                 │
            
                         Compile Program
            
                                 │
            
                                 ▼
            
                           Kernel Verifier
            
                                 │
            
                      Safe Program Approved
            
                                 │
            
                                 ▼
            
                          eBPF Program
            
                                 │
            
                         Kernel Hook Point
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # eBPF Datapath
            
            Instead of relying on:
            
            ```text
            iptables
            
            ↓
            
            Rule Chains
            ```
            
            or
            
            ```text
            IPVS
            
            ↓
            
            Kernel Tables
            ```
            
            the packet enters an eBPF program.
            
            ---
            
            # High-Level Datapath
            
            ```text
            Packet
            
            ↓
            
            eBPF Hook
            
            ↓
            
            Service Lookup
            
            ↓
            
            Backend Selection
            
            ↓
            
            Forward Packet
            ```
            
            No long rule chains are required.
            
            ---
            
            # Complete Architecture
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            NIC
            
            ↓
            
            Kernel
            
            ↓
            
            eBPF Program
            
            ↓
            
            Service Lookup
            
            ↓
            
            Backend Pod
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Why is This Faster?
            
            Traditional iptables:
            
            ```text
            Packet
            
            ↓
            
            Evaluate Rules
            
            ↓
            
            Find Match
            ```
            
            eBPF:
            
            ```text
            Packet
            
            ↓
            
            Hash Lookup
            
            ↓
            
            Backend
            ```
            
            Fewer processing steps can reduce latency and CPU overhead.
            
            ---
            
            # Hook Points
            
            eBPF programs execute at predefined hook points inside the kernel.
            
            The two most important networking hooks are:
            
            ```text
            XDP
            
            TC
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # XDP
            
            XDP stands for:
            
            ```text
            Express Data Path
            ```
            
            It executes extremely early.
            
            Packet flow:
            
            ```text
            NIC
            
            ↓
            
            XDP
            
            ↓
            
            Kernel Network Stack
            ```
            
            This allows packet processing before much of the traditional networking stack executes.
            
            ---
            
            # Why XDP?
            
            Early processing allows:
            
            - Early packet drops
            - Early forwarding
            - DDoS mitigation
            - High-performance packet handling
            
            ---
            
            # XDP Workflow
            
            ```text
            Packet
            
            ↓
            
            NIC Driver
            
            ↓
            
            XDP
            
            ↓
            
            Decision
            
            ↓
            
            Drop
            
            Pass
            
            Redirect
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # TC Hooks
            
            Another important hook is:
            
            ```text
            Traffic Control
            
            (TC)
            ```
            
            Unlike XDP,
            
            TC executes later in the networking pipeline.
            
            ---
            
            # TC Architecture
            
            ```text
            Packet
            
            ↓
            
            Kernel Stack
            
            ↓
            
            TC Hook
            
            ↓
            
            eBPF Program
            
            ↓
            
            Continue
            ```
            
            TC provides access after more networking context has been established.
            
            ---
            
            # XDP vs TC
            
            | XDP | TC |
            |------|----|
            | Earliest hook | Later hook |
            | Driver level | Network stack level |
            | Extremely fast | More networking context available |
            | Limited packet context | Rich packet metadata |
            
            ---
            
            # BPF Maps
            
            Programs need memory.
            
            They cannot use ordinary kernel variables.
            
            Instead,
            
            they use:
            
            ```text
            BPF Maps
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # What are BPF Maps?
            
            BPF Maps are shared kernel data structures.
            
            They store:
            
            - Service information
            - Endpoint lists
            - Connection state
            - Statistics
            - Configuration
            
            Think of them as extremely fast kernel key-value stores.
            
            ---
            
            # Architecture
            
            ```text
            eBPF Program
            
            ↓
            
            Lookup
            
            ↓
            
            BPF Map
            
            ↓
            
            Result
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Example
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Service IP
            
            ↓
            
            10.96.0.15
            
            ↓
            
            Lookup
            
            ↓
            
            Backend List
            ```
            
            The Service definition exists inside a BPF Map.
            
            ---
            
            # Types of Maps
            
            Common map types include:
            
            - Hash Maps
            - Array Maps
            - LRU Hash Maps
            - Per-CPU Maps
            - Ring Buffers
            
            Networking solutions such as Cilium use several map types simultaneously.
            
            ---
            
            # Service Lookup
            
            Suppose:
            
            ```text
            Destination
            
            10.96.0.15
            ```
            
            Instead of:
            
            ```text
            iptables
            
            ↓
            
            Rule Chain
            ```
            
            the eBPF program performs:
            
            ```text
            Service IP
            
            ↓
            
            BPF Map Lookup
            
            ↓
            
            Backend List
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # Internal Workflow
            
            ```text
            Packet
            
            ↓
            
            Extract Service IP
            
            ↓
            
            Lookup BPF Map
            
            ↓
            
            Return Backends
            
            ↓
            
            Choose Backend
            ```
            
            The lookup is efficient and avoids traversing rule chains.
            
            ---
            
            # Load Balancing
            
            Once backends are found,
            
            the eBPF dataplane selects one.
            
            Conceptually:
            
            ```text
            Backend List
            
            ↓
            
            Selection
            
            ↓
            
            Chosen Pod
            ```
            
            The exact selection algorithm depends on the implementation.
            
            ---
            
            # Connection Tracking
            
            eBPF implementations still require connection tracking.
            
            Modern dataplanes may:
            
            - Integrate with Linux conntrack
            - Maintain their own connection-tracking mechanisms
            - Use a combination of both
            
            The exact behavior depends on the implementation (for example, Cilium supports both approaches depending on the feature and deployment mode).
            
            ---
            
            # Direct Server Return (DSR)
            
            Traditional networking often returns packets through the Node that received them.
            
            Example:
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Node A
            
            ↓
            
            Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Node A
            
            ↓
            
            Client
            ```
            
            This introduces an extra return hop.
            
            ---
            
            # What is DSR?
            
            DSR stands for:
            
            ```text
            Direct Server Return
            ```
            
            Instead of returning through the ingress Node,
            
            the backend responds directly.
            
            ---
            
            # Traditional Response
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Node
            
            ↓
            
            Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Node
            
            ↓
            
            Client
            ```
            
            ---
            
            # DSR Response
            
            ```text
            Client
            
            ↓
            
            Node
            
            ↓
            
            Pod
            
            ↓
            
            Client
            ```
            
            The response bypasses the original forwarding Node.
            
            ---
            
            # Advantages
            
            - Lower latency
            - Reduced Node bandwidth
            - Better scalability
            - Reduced CPU utilization
            
            ---
            
            # kube-proxy-free Kubernetes
            
            One of the biggest innovations:
            
            ```text
            No kube-proxy
            ```
            
            The traditional dataplane disappears.
            
            Instead:
            
            ```text
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            Networking Agent
            
            ↓
            
            eBPF Programs
            
            ↓
            
            Kernel
            ```
            
            Service implementation happens directly through eBPF.
            
            ---
            
            # Architecture Comparison
            
            Traditional:
            
            ```text
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            kube-proxy
            
            ↓
            
            iptables/IPVS
            
            ↓
            
            Kernel
            ```
            
            Modern:
            
            ```text
            API Server
            
            ↓
            
            eBPF Agent
            
            ↓
            
            Kernel Programs
            ```
            
            No kube-proxy rule management is required.
            
            ---
            
